# Tidewater On-Disk Format @tidewater-format
{
    @meta {
        type: doc

        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    @about {
        The current on-disk format of tidewater, format version 6, byte by byte: the conventions every file shares, the write-ahead log, the naming of flushed tables, SSTables and their blocks, the manifest and its version edits, checkpoint records, and how format versions 3 to 6 are told apart, upgraded and downgraded, with the checks a reader applies and the error each failure produces. Read it when implementing a reader, a writer or a tool for tidewater's files, when changing anything the engine writes to disk, or when a hex dump of a database file needs explaining. It specifies bytes, not behaviour: when the engine writes, fsyncs, compacts and recovers is outside its scope.
    }

    @reading-guide {
        The sections follow the files of a database in the order a write reaches them: the log, then a flushed table, then the manifest edit that records it. Each stands on its own once @conventions is read.

        {[table]
            Sections | Note
            @scope, @conventions | First: what is specified here, byte order, integer encodings, checksums and file names
            @write-ahead-log-format | For reading, verifying or writing log segments
            @flush-and-naming, @sstable-format | For tables: how they are named and published, and their blocks
            @manifest-format, @checkpoint-records | For the list of live tables and the point where replay starts
            @format-versions | Before reading files written by another release, or changing the format
            @reader-conformance | As a checklist when implementing a compatible reader
            @appendix-constants, @appendix-tags, @appendix-errors, @appendix-test-vectors | As references while implementing
            @appendix-field-index, @appendix-mistakes, @appendix-go | To find a field by name, or the cause of a failed vector
        }
    }

    # Scope @scope
    {
        This document specifies format version 6, the version the engine writes, which is the value of `TIDEWATER_FORMAT_VERSION` in `src/format.go`. The engine reads format versions 3 to 6. What each version added, and what a reader of an older file does differently, is in @format-versions.

        It specifies the bytes of every file the engine keeps in a database directory and the checks a reader applies to them. When the engine writes, fsyncs, flushes, compacts or recovers belongs to [the architecture](../architecture.sdoc), and this document links to it instead of repeating it. Every figure that tunes the engine lives there only: the fsync thresholds of the log, the size at which a log segment is closed, the checkpoint interval, the default bits per key of the bloom filters, and the target size of a table. A reader never needs any of them to decode a file.

        {[table]
            Question | Where it is answered
            Which bytes does a file hold, and how is each field checked? | This document
            Why is the format the way it is? | [Tidewater Architecture](../architecture.sdoc) and [the decisions log](../decisions.sdoc)
            How does the engine use a part of the format at run time? | The component documents, such as [SSTables](../components/sstables.sdoc), [Manifest](../components/manifest.sdoc) and [Bloom Filters](../components/bloom-filters.sdoc)
            What did format version 3 look like when release 1.0 wrote it? | [Tidewater On-Disk Format, Version 3](format-v1.sdoc), superseded and kept for its history
        }

        # Terms Used Here @spec-terms
        {
            {[table]
                Term | Meaning in this document
                Reader | Any program that decodes tidewater's files: the engine on open, `tidewater-admin`, a test, or a tool written by someone else
                Writer | The engine, or a tool such as `tidewater-admin downgrade` that writes files in place of it
                Must, must not | A requirement. A reader or writer that breaks it is not compatible with this format
                Should | A recommendation that a compatible reader may depart from with a reason
                Refuse | Stop with an error and change nothing on disk
                Corrupt | Failing a check of this document. Unless a section says otherwise, a reader returns an error wrapping `ErrCorrupt` for it
            }

            The glossary defines the words the whole project uses, such as memtable, level and tombstone: see [the glossary](../glossary.sdoc).
        }
    }

    # Conventions @conventions
    {
        The rules in this section hold in every file of a database, for every format version this document covers. A section below that departs from one of them says so.

        # Notation @notation
        {
            Fields are given in tables with these columns, in this order: the offset of the field from the start of the structure, its size in bytes, its name, its encoding, and what it means. An offset or a size that depends on an earlier field is written as an expression in that field's name, such as `17 + length`.

            Byte strings are written in hexadecimal, two digits per byte, in the order they appear in the file, separated by spaces: `fa f0 16 68`. Integers are written in decimal unless they carry the prefix `0x`. A hex dump gives the offset of its first byte in hexadecimal on the left, sixteen bytes per line, and the bytes as ASCII on the right, with a dot for any byte that is not printable:

            ```
            00000000  54 49 44 45 2d 53 53 54                          TIDE-SST
            ```

            Byte strings quoted from text, such as a property name, are written in backquotes and stand for their UTF-8 bytes, without a terminating zero.
        }

        # Byte Order @byte-order
        {
            Every fixed-width integer in every file is little-endian: the least significant byte comes first. There is no exception, not in the log, not in the tables, not in the manifest.

            ```
            uint32 0x00000011 is written    11 00 00 00
            uint64 0x00000000000003e8 is    e8 03 00 00 00 00 00 00
            ```

            Checksums are integers too, and are written the same way. A CRC32C of `0x6816f0fa` appears in a file as `fa f0 16 68`, which is how a hex dump shows it; tools print the integer, as `0x6816f0fa`.
        }

        # Fixed-Width Integers @fixed-width-integers
        {
            {[table]
                Name | Size | Range | Go encoder
                `uint8` | 1 | 0 to 255 | a single byte
                `uint32` | 4 | 0 to 4,294,967,295 | `binary.LittleEndian.PutUint32`
                `uint64` | 8 | 0 to 2^64 − 1 | `binary.LittleEndian.PutUint64`
            }

            No field of the format is signed. Where a quantity could in principle be negative, such as an expiry time before the Unix epoch, the format does not represent it, and a writer refuses the value before it reaches the disk.
        }

        # Varints @varints
        {
            A varint is an unsigned integer of up to 64 bits in the encoding of Go's `binary.PutUvarint`, also known as unsigned LEB128. The integer is cut into groups of 7 bits, least significant group first. Every group is written as one byte whose high bit is set when another byte follows and clear on the last byte.

            {[table]
                Value | Bytes
                0 | `00`
                1 | `01`
                127 | `7f`
                128 | `80 01`
                300 | `ac 02`
                16,383 | `ff 7f`
                16,384 | `80 80 01`
                32,751 | `ef ff 01`
                1,284,733 | `fd b4 4e`
                4,294,967,295 | `ff ff ff ff 0f`
                2^64 − 1 | `ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff 01`
            }

            A varint is at most 10 bytes long. A reader must treat as corrupt a varint that has more than 10 bytes, whose tenth byte is greater than 1, or that runs past the end of the structure holding it. These are the three errors `binary.Uvarint` reports, and a reader written in Go gets the checks for free by testing its second result for zero or a negative number.

            A writer always writes the shortest encoding. A reader accepts a longer one, such as `80 00` for 0, as Go's decoder does: no check in this document depends on the length of a varint, and refusing a padded varint would refuse files that decode to exactly the same values.

            Where a field is a varint but the value it holds is bounded more tightly, such as a level number or a compression type, the field's own section gives the bound, and a value beyond it is corrupt even though it decodes.
        }

        # Length-Prefixed Bytes @length-prefixed-bytes
        {
            Length-prefixed bytes are a varint length followed by exactly that many bytes. The bytes are opaque unless the field says otherwise: keys and values are arbitrary bytes, and nothing in the format assumes they are text.

            ```
            "key1" as length-prefixed bytes    04 6b 65 79 31
            an empty value                     00
            ```

            A length that runs past the end of the enclosing structure, a record payload, a block, or a manifest edit, makes the structure corrupt. A reader must check the length against what is left of the enclosing structure before it allocates anything, so that a damaged length can never make it allocate gigabytes.
        }

        # Checksums @checksums
        {
            Two checksum functions are used, each in its own place. Neither is masked, salted or seeded with anything but its standard initial value: a checksum in a file is exactly what the standard function returns over the bytes the field's section names.

            # CRC32C @crc32c
            {
                CRC32C is the 32-bit cyclic redundancy check with the Castagnoli polynomial, `0x1EDC6F41`, or `0x82F63B78` in its reflected form, with an initial value of all ones and a final inversion. It is the function Go computes with `crc32.Checksum(data, crc32.MakeTable(crc32.Castagnoli))`, which is how `src/wal.go` computes it, and the one the SSE 4.2 and ARMv8 CRC instructions implement.

                {[table]
                    Input | CRC32C
                    The 9 bytes `123456789` | `0xe3069283`
                    32 bytes of `00` | `0x8a9136aa`
                    32 bytes of `ff` | `0x62a8ab43`
                    The 32 bytes `00 01 02` up to `1f` | `0x46dd794e`
                    No bytes at all | `0x00000000`
                }

                The check values are the standard ones, and an implementation that does not reproduce them is not CRC32C. A common mistake is to use the IEEE polynomial of `crc32.ChecksumIEEE`, gzip and zip: it gives `0xcbf43926` for `123456789` and fails every check in this document.

                CRC32C protects every log record and every SSTable block, as [D-0004](../decisions.sdoc#d-0004) decided after a torn write at the tail of the log was found to replay as garbage.
            }

            # xxh3-64 @xxh3-64
            {
                xxh3-64 is the 64-bit variant of the XXH3 hash from the xxHash family, with seed 0 and the default secret. In Go it is `xxh3.Hash(data)` from the pure Go package the engine vendors; there is no cgo in it, as [D-0001](../decisions.sdoc#d-0001) requires of every dependency.

                {[table]
                    Input | xxh3-64
                    No bytes at all | `0x2d06800538d394c2`
                    The 9 bytes `123456789` | `0x72dcb18b67a17dff`
                    The 9 bytes `tidewater` | `0x42618d12d77a2315`
                }

                xxh3-64 protects every manifest edit, and it is also the hash the bloom filters probe with. In a filter it is a hash, not a checksum: nothing compares it with a stored value.

                The manifest is outside D-0004 on purpose. A manifest edit that replays wrongly loses whole tables rather than one key range, and a 64-bit checksum makes an undetected corruption of an edit far less likely than a 32-bit one would. The reasoning is set out in [Manifest](../components/manifest.sdoc#version-edits).
            }

            # Which Checksum Covers What @checksum-coverage
            {
                {[table]
                    Structure | Function | Stored as | Covers | Section
                    Log record | CRC32C | `uint32`, first field of the header | The type, the sequence number and the payload, not the length | [Write-Ahead Log Format](#write-ahead-log-format)
                    SSTable block | CRC32C | `uint32`, last field of the block trailer | The block's bytes as stored, then the compression byte | @block-trailer
                    SSTable footer | none | | Checked by its magic number and the bounds of its handles | @sstable-footer
                    Manifest edit | xxh3-64 | `uint64`, second field of the edit header | The edit's body, not its length | @edit-framing
                    `CURRENT` | none | | Checked by its syntax | @current-file
                }

                Wherever a checksum does not cover the length that delimits it, a damaged length makes the checksum run over the wrong bytes, and the check fails all the same. The probability that a random error passes a CRC32C is one in 2^32, and an xxh3-64 one in 2^64; the format relies on nothing stronger.
            }
        }

        # Sizes and Units @units
        {
            A KiB is 1,024 bytes and a MiB is 1,048,576 bytes. Sizes in fields are always in bytes. A size in a field never includes the field itself or the header that holds it, unless its section says so.

            Times in fields are milliseconds since the Unix epoch, as Go's `time.Time.UnixMilli` returns them, stored as a `uint64` or as a varint, and are compared with the wall clock of the node reading them. Only expiry times are stored, so this is the only kind of time the format holds.
        }

        # File Numbers @file-numbers
        {
            Every file of a database except `CURRENT` and `LOCK` carries a file number in its name. File numbers are unsigned 64-bit integers. They start at 1, and they come from a single counter shared by log segments, tables and manifests, so no two files of one database ever have the same number, whatever their kind.

            A number is never reused, even when the file that had it is deleted, and even when the file never made it into the manifest. The manifest records the next unused number as `next-file-number`, and on open the engine takes the larger of that value and one more than the highest number of any file in the directory, so a number that a crash left on an orphan is not handed out again.

            In a file name the number is written in decimal, zero-padded to six digits: file 42 is `000042`, file 951 is `000951`. A number of more than six digits is written with as many digits as it needs and no padding, so file 1,048,576 is `1048576`. A reader must accept any number of digits from six up, and must treat a name of fewer than six digits, or with a sign or any character that is not a digit, as not a tidewater file.

            Names sort by number only when their digits are compared as numbers. A reader that lists segments must parse the numbers and sort them, never sort the names as strings: `1000000.log` sorts before `999999.log` as a string.
        }

        # Files of a Database @database-files
        {
            A database is a directory. The engine creates two subdirectories in it, `wal/` for log segments and `sst/` for tables, and nothing deeper.

            ```
            db/
              CURRENT              name of the live manifest
              LOCK                 held while the database is open
              MANIFEST-000042      version edits
              wal/000950.log       log segment
              sst/000951.sst       SSTable
            ```

            {[table]
                Name | Kind | Written | Specified in
                `CURRENT` | Pointer to the live manifest | Whole, through `CURRENT.tmp` and a rename | @current-file
                `CURRENT.tmp` | `CURRENT` being replaced | Exists only during a rename | @current-file
                `LOCK` | Lock file, empty | Created on open, locked with `flock` | This section
                `MANIFEST-NNNNNN` | Version edits | Appended | @manifest-format
                `wal/NNNNNN.log` | Log segment | Appended | [Write-Ahead Log Format](#write-ahead-log-format)
                `sst/NNNNNN.sst` | SSTable | Whole, through a temporary name and a rename | @sstable-format
                `sst/NNNNNN.sst.tmp` | SSTable being written | Exists only until its rename | @flush-publish
            }

            `LOCK` holds no data. The engine opens it, creating it if needed, and takes an exclusive `flock` on it for as long as the database is open; a second process that tries to open the database fails with `tidewater: database is locked by another process`. A clean `Close` removes the file after releasing the lock, so a `LOCK` file found unlocked on open means the last close was not clean, which the engine logs at warning level as `stale lock found: last close was not clean`.

            A reader that only inspects files, such as `tidewater-admin manifest dump`, must not take the lock and must not create `LOCK`, so that it can run beside an open database.

            Any other file in the directory or in its two subdirectories is not part of the database. The engine leaves it alone and logs its name once at open, at info level. In particular, a backup tool's or an editor's stray files are never deleted.
        }

        # Temporary Files and Renames @temporary-files
        {
            A file that is written whole, a table or `CURRENT`, is written under a temporary name, fsynced, and renamed to its final name; the directory is then fsynced so that the rename itself is durable. A reader therefore sees either the complete file under its final name or nothing under that name.

            A file that is appended to, a log segment or a manifest, is created under its final name and grows. Its tail can be torn by a crash, and its own section says what a reader does with a torn tail: the log and the manifest have different rules, given in @log-torn-tail and @manifest-torn-tail.

            {[table]
                File | Temporary name | Left behind by a crash | Removed by
                Table | `sst/NNNNNN.sst.tmp` | A partial or complete table no edit names | The next open, as in @orphans
                `CURRENT` | `CURRENT.tmp` | A pointer that was never installed | The next open
                Manifest | none, written under its final name | A manifest `CURRENT` does not name | The next open
                Log segment | none, written under its final name | A torn tail | Recovery, as in @log-torn-tail
            }
        }

        # Reading Files @reading-files
        {
            A reader reads every file with explicit reads at given offsets, `pread` or Go's `ReadAt`, and never maps a file into memory: a disk error must reach the reader as an error value, not as a signal, which is why the engine rejected memory-mapped tables, as [Memory-Mapped Tables](../architecture.sdoc#rejected-mmap) explains.

            {[table]
                File | How it is read
                Table | Footer first, at the file's size minus 60; then each block through its handle, block and trailer in one read
                Log segment | From the start, in reads of whole 32 KiB blocks, so that a header never needs a second read
                Manifest | Whole, from the start, once per open
                `CURRENT` | Whole; it is a few bytes long
            }

            A short read, fewer bytes than asked for where the file is long enough, is retried, never taken as the end of the file. A file's size is taken once, before it is read: a log segment or a manifest may grow while a tool reads it, and the tool reads only up to the size it took, treating whatever lies there as the tail.

            Tables are immutable and may be read by any number of readers at once. A segment or a manifest being appended to may be read too, by a tool that does not take `LOCK`, and its tail can then look torn because the append is still in flight; such a tool reports a torn tail as possibly in progress, never as damage, unless the database is closed.
        }
    }

    # Write-Ahead Log Format @write-ahead-log-format
    {
        The log is a sequence of segment files under `wal/`, each a sequence of 32 KiB blocks holding records. A record carries one batch, or a fragment of one, behind a 17-byte header with a CRC32C. This section specifies those bytes and what a reader does with them, including with a segment whose tail is torn or damaged.

        When the bytes reach the disk is not part of the format. The fsync thresholds, the grouping of concurrent writers into one append, what `Sync: true` waits for, and the size at which a segment is closed are given in [Write-Ahead Log](../architecture.sdoc#write-ahead-log), and nothing a reader does depends on them. A segment written under any fsync policy decodes the same way.

        The log carries no format version of its own. The layout below is the one every release that writes format version 6 reads and writes. Of the additions listed in @format-versions, the one that reaches the log is the expiry time of format 6, carried by entries of kind 2.

        # Segments @log-segments
        {
            A segment is the file `wal/NNNNNN.log`, named after a file number from the database's single counter, as in @file-numbers. A new database's first segment is `wal/000001.log`.

            A segment has no header, no trailer and no index. Its first record starts at offset 0, and its length is the end of its last record, or of the padding after it; it is not rounded up to a whole block, so the last block of a segment is usually short. An empty segment, zero bytes long, is valid: it is what a segment looks like between its creation and the first append.

            Every segment belongs to one memtable. When the active memtable is frozen, the engine closes the current segment and opens the next one under a new number, so the records of two memtables never share a segment. A closed segment is never appended to again. Its tail is left as the last append left it, with no padding to the end of its block and no end marker; a reader tells a closed segment from the active one only by whether a segment with a higher number exists.

            Segments are read in ascending order of their numbers, parsed as integers. Replay starts at the segment numbered by the manifest's `log-number`, as @replay-start describes, and segments numbered below it hold nothing replay needs.
        }

        # Blocks @log-blocks
        {
            A segment is divided into blocks of exactly 32,768 bytes: block i covers the offsets from 32,768 × i up to, but not including, 32,768 × (i + 1). Blocks are a property of offsets, not of the bytes: nothing in the file marks where one begins.

            No record crosses a block boundary. A batch too large for what is left of the current block is split into fragments, as @log-fragmentation describes, so that every block starts with a record header or with the continuation of nothing at all.

            A writer starts a record only where at least 18 bytes are left in the block: the 17 bytes of a header and at least one byte of payload. When 17 bytes or fewer are left, it fills them with zeros and starts the next record at the next block boundary. The padding is 1 to 17 bytes of `00`, never more.

            ```
            block 0                                               block 1
            ... record ending at 32,758 | 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 | record at 32,768 ...
                                          10 bytes of padding
            ```

            A reader skips the padding without looking at it: when fewer than 18 bytes are left in the current block, it moves to the next block boundary. A reader must not treat the padding as a record, and must not report nonzero bytes there as damage, since nothing in the format protects them.

            The blocks are what lets a tool find record boundaries past damage: whatever happened earlier in a segment, a record or a fragment starts at every block boundary. Replay itself never resynchronises this way, because it stops at the first bad record, as @log-torn-tail explains; only a tool that looks past damage on purpose has a use for the boundaries.
        }

        # Record Header @log-record-header
        {
            Every record starts with a header of 17 bytes, followed by its payload. The layout is the one in [Record Format](../architecture.sdoc#wal-format):

            ```
            +---------+---------+----------+---------+-----------------+
            | crc32c  | length  | type     | seqno   | payload         |
            | 4 bytes | 4 bytes | 1 byte   | 8 bytes | length bytes    |
            +---------+---------+----------+---------+-----------------+
            ```

            {[table]
                Offset | Size | Field | Encoding | Meaning
                0 | 4 | crc32c | `uint32` | CRC32C of the bytes from offset 8 to the end of the payload: the type, the sequence number and the payload
                4 | 4 | length | `uint32` | Length of this record's payload in bytes, from 1 to 32,751
                8 | 1 | type | `uint8` | Record type, as in @log-record-types
                9 | 8 | seqno | `uint64` | Sequence number of the first entry of the batch this record carries, as in @log-sequence-numbers
                17 | length | payload | bytes | The whole batch, or one fragment of it
            }

            The checksum covers the type, the sequence number and the payload, which are contiguous: a reader computes it over the `9 + length` bytes that start at offset 8. It does not cover the length field. A damaged length makes the checksum run over the wrong bytes, so it fails all the same, unless the damaged length points past the block or the file, which the reader checks first.

            The length field has room for far more than a record can hold. A record never crosses a block boundary, so its payload is at most 32,768 − 17 = 32,751 bytes, and a record that starts after the beginning of a block holds less. A length of 0 is never written, since a writer starts a record only where it can give it at least one byte, and a reader treats it as corrupt.

            The function that lays a full record out is `encode` in `src/wal.go`. It writes the length at offset 4, the type at 8 and the sequence number at 9, copies the payload to 17, and puts the CRC32C of everything from offset 8 on at offset 0.
        }

        # Record Types @log-record-types
        {
            {[table]
                Value | Name | Meaning
                0 | none | Never a record type. Zero bytes where a header should be are padding when fewer than 18 bytes are left in the block, and a bad record otherwise
                1 | `full` | A whole batch in one record
                2 | `first` | The first fragment of a batch, which runs to the end of its block
                3 | `middle` | A fragment that fills a whole block, between a `first` and a `last`
                4 | `last` | The last fragment of a batch, starting at a block boundary
                5 to 255 | none | Reserved. A record of one of these types is a bad record
            }

            A batch is either one `full` record or a sequence of one `first`, zero or more `middle` and one `last` record, with nothing between them but the block boundaries they straddle. Its payload is the concatenation of the fragments' payloads, in order.
        }

        # Fragmentation @log-fragmentation
        {
            A writer appends a batch whose encoding is `n` bytes long as follows, with `left` the number of bytes left in the current block:

            {[#]
                - If `left` is less than 18, pad the rest of the block with zeros and continue at the next block boundary, where `left` is 32,768.
                - If `n` is at most `left − 17`, write one `full` record holding the whole batch. Done.
                - Otherwise write a `first` record holding the first `left − 17` bytes, which fills the block exactly.
                - While more than 32,751 bytes remain, write a `middle` record holding the next 32,751 bytes, which fills the next block exactly.
                - Write a `last` record holding the rest, at the start of the next block.
            }

            A writer must not move a batch to the next block to avoid splitting it. Splitting wherever 18 bytes or more are left is what makes the layout of a log a function of the batches alone, so that the test vectors in @appendix-test-vectors are byte-exact, and it wastes at most 17 bytes per block.

            Every fragment of a batch carries the same sequence number in its header, the batch's, and each fragment's CRC32C covers its own type, sequence number and payload. A reader can therefore check each fragment on its own before it has seen the others.

            The structure gives a reader four checks that cost nothing: a `first` must end exactly at a block boundary, a `middle` must start at one and end at the next, a `last` must start at one, and every fragment after the `first` must carry the `first` one's sequence number. A fragment that breaks any of them is a bad record, of the kind `log.fragment` in @log-validation.
        }

        # Batch Encoding @log-batch-encoding
        {
            The payload of a `full` record, or the concatenation of a batch's fragments, is one batch. A single `Put` or `Delete` is written as a batch of one entry, so every logical record in the log is a batch.

            {[table]
                Offset | Size | Field | Encoding | Meaning
                0 | 4 | count | `uint32` | Number of entries in the batch, at least 1
                4 | rest | entries | `count` entries, as below | In the order the application added them
            }

            {[table]
                Field | Encoding | Present for | Meaning
                kind | `uint8` | Every entry | 0 for a delete, 1 for a put, 2 for a put with an expiry time
                key | length-prefixed bytes | Every entry | The user key
                expiry | `uint64` | Kind 2 only | When the value expires, in milliseconds since the Unix epoch
                value | length-prefixed bytes | Kinds 1 and 2 | The value
            }

            The kinds are the same values the tables use in their internal keys, listed once in @entry-kinds. A put without an expiry is always written as kind 1, never as kind 2 with some special expiry: a reader must not interpret any expiry value as "never".

            The entries must use the payload exactly. A batch whose count is 0, whose entry has a kind other than 0, 1 or 2, whose key or value runs past the end of the payload, or that leaves bytes after its last entry, is a bad record of the kind `log.batch`.

            The size of a batch is bounded by the engine, not by the format: a batch never exceeds the size of a memtable, as [Batches and Snapshots](../architecture.sdoc#batches) explains, and a larger one is refused with `ErrBatchTooLarge` before anything is written. A reader does not check the bound, which a change of `MemtableSize` between two opens can move.

            Keys may be empty and values may be empty; neither is padded or terminated. The order of the entries matters: two entries of one batch for the same key are both applied, in order, and the later one, with the higher sequence number, is the one reads see.
        }

        # Sequence Numbers @log-sequence-numbers
        {
            The header's `seqno` is the sequence number of the batch's first entry. Entry `i` of the batch, counting from 0, has the sequence number `seqno + i`, so a batch of `count` entries occupies the numbers from `seqno` to `seqno + count − 1`, and the next batch in the log starts at `seqno + count`.

            Sequence numbers are assigned when a record enters the log, in log order, which is therefore the order of visibility. The number 0 is never assigned: the first write to a new database gets 1. On a follower, the records the applier appends keep the leader's sequence numbers, so a follower's log has the same numbers as the leader's even though its segments differ.

            Within the records a reader replays, the batches must be contiguous: each one's `seqno` equals the previous batch's `seqno + count`, across fragment and segment boundaries alike. A batch that does not is a bad record of the kind `log.sequence`. The first batch replayed may start anywhere above the manifest's `checkpoint`, since flushed segments before it have been deleted, as @replay-start describes.

            A sequence number must be lower than 2^56: the tables pack it into 56 bits of the internal key, as @internal-keys shows. The engine refuses writes long before the counter gets there, and a reader treats a larger number in the log as a bad record of the kind `log.sequence`.
        }

        # Grouped Appends @log-group-commit
        {
            The log writer groups the batches of concurrent writers into one append, but every batch keeps its own record, or its own fragments: grouping changes how many system calls write the bytes, never the bytes. Three writers whose single-entry batches were grouped, a put of `a`, a delete of `b` and a put of `c`, given the sequence numbers 41, 42 and 43 and appended at offset 8,192:

            ```
            00002000  1b 13 ba f3 09 00 00 00 01 29 00 00 00 00 00 00  .........)......
            00002010  00 01 00 00 00 01 01 61 01 31 4b 85 73 7f 07 00  .......a.1K.s...
            00002020  00 00 01 2a 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00  ...*............
            00002030  01 62 5f 89 27 e2 09 00 00 00 01 2b 00 00 00 00  .b_.'......+....
            00002040  00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 01 63 01 33              .........c.3
            ```

            {[table]
                Offset | Record | Payload | CRC32C
                8,192 | `full`, sequence number 41: put of `a` to `1` | 9 bytes | `0xf3ba131b`
                8,218 | `full`, sequence number 42: delete of `b` | 7 bytes | `0x7f73854b`
                8,242 | `full`, sequence number 43: put of `c` to `3` | 9 bytes | `0xe227895f`
            }

            A reader cannot tell grouped batches from batches appended one at a time, and must not need to. A crash during a grouped append can tear any of its records, and the reader keeps every batch before the tear, whichever writer it came from. The writers of a group are woken only once the append, and the fsync if any of them asked for one, is done, so no writer that asked for `Sync: true` was told its write was durable when the group is torn.
        }

        # Example: A Full Record @log-example-full
        {
            The first write to a new database, `db.Put([]byte("key1"), []byte("value1"), tidewater.WriteOptions{})`, is a batch of one put with sequence number 1. Its payload is 17 bytes:

            ```
            01 00 00 00                      count = 1
            01                               kind = 1, put
            04 6b 65 79 31                   key "key1"
            06 76 61 6c 75 65 31             value "value1"
            ```

            It fits at offset 0 of `wal/000001.log`, so it is written as one `full` record of 34 bytes:

            ```
            00000000  fa f0 16 68 11 00 00 00 01 01 00 00 00 00 00 00  ...h............
            00000010  00 01 00 00 00 01 04 6b 65 79 31 06 76 61 6c 75  .......key1.valu
            00000020  65 31                                            e1
            ```

            {[table]
                Bytes | Field | Value
                `fa f0 16 68` | crc32c | `0x6816f0fa`, the CRC32C of the 26 bytes from offset 8 to 33
                `11 00 00 00` | length | 17
                `01` | type | `full`
                `01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00` | seqno | 1
                `01 00 00 00` up to `65 31` | payload | The 17 bytes above
            }

            The next record starts at offset 34, with 32,734 bytes left in block 0.
        }

        # Example: A Batch of Three Entries @log-example-batch
        {
            A batch holding a put of `user:42`, a delete of `user:7` and a put of `session:9` that expires at 1,900,000,000,000 milliseconds, written with sequence number 1,000 as a `full` record at the start of a block:

            ```
            00000000  40 48 ff 3c 32 00 00 00 01 e8 03 00 00 00 00 00  @H.<2...........
            00000010  00 03 00 00 00 01 07 75 73 65 72 3a 34 32 05 61  .......user:42.a
            00000020  6c 69 63 65 00 06 75 73 65 72 3a 37 02 09 73 65  lice..user:7..se
            00000030  73 73 69 6f 6e 3a 39 00 38 d3 60 ba 01 00 00 03  ssion:9.8.`.....
            00000040  74 6f 6b                                         tok
            ```

            {[table]
                Offset | Bytes | Meaning
                0 | `40 48 ff 3c` | CRC32C `0x3cff4840`
                4 | `32 00 00 00` | Payload of 50 bytes
                8 | `01` | `full`
                9 | `e8 03 00 00 00 00 00 00` | Sequence number 1,000
                17 | `03 00 00 00` | Three entries
                21 | `01 07` and `user:42`, `05` and `alice` | Put of `user:42`, sequence number 1,000
                37 | `00 06` and `user:7` | Delete of `user:7`, sequence number 1,001
                44 | `02 09` and `session:9` | Put with expiry of `session:9`, sequence number 1,002
                55 | `00 38 d3 60 ba 01 00 00` | Its expiry, 1,900,000,000,000 as a `uint64`
                63 | `03` and `tok` | Its value
            }

            The batch occupies sequence numbers 1,000 to 1,002, so the next batch in the log must carry 1,003.
        }

        # Example: A Fragmented Batch @log-example-fragmented
        {
            A batch holding one put of the key `big` with a value of 40,000 bytes of `x`, sequence number 5,000, appended when the previous record ended at offset 32,700 of block 0. Its encoding is 40,012 bytes, which begins:

            ```
            01 00 00 00 01 03 62 69 67 c0 b8 02 78 78 78 78 ...
            count=1     put key "big"   len 40,000  value
            ```

            Block 0 has 68 bytes left, room for a header and 51 bytes, so the batch is split into three records:

            {[table]
                Offset | Type | Payload | Header bytes | CRC32C
                32,700 | `first` | 51 bytes, the encoding's first 51 | `96 be 46 be 33 00 00 00 02 88 13 00 00 00 00 00 00` | `0xbe46be96`
                32,768 | `middle` | 32,751 bytes, filling block 1 | `4d 7c d7 7b ef 7f 00 00 03 88 13 00 00 00 00 00 00` | `0x7bd77c4d`
                65,536 | `last` | 7,210 bytes, the rest | `f3 a0 26 e3 2a 1c 00 00 04 88 13 00 00 00 00 00 00` | `0xe326a0f3`
            }

            The three payloads add up to 51 + 32,751 + 7,210 = 40,012 bytes. All three headers carry the sequence number 5,000, `88 13` followed by six zeros, and the next record starts at offset 72,763, in block 2, with the sequence number 5,001.

            Had the previous record ended at offset 32,751 or later, 17 bytes or fewer would have been left in block 0: they would have been padded, and the batch would have started at offset 32,768 with a `first` record of 32,751 bytes, followed by a `last` of 7,261.
        }

        # Example: Padding at the End of a Block @log-example-padding
        {
            A record whose payload ends at offset 32,758 leaves 10 bytes in block 0, fewer than the 18 a new record needs. The writer fills them with zeros and starts the next record, a put of `key1` to `value1` with sequence number 7,000, at offset 32,768:

            ```
            00007fe8  78 78 78 78 78 78 78 78 78 78 78 78 78 78 00 00  xxxxxxxxxxxxxx..
            00007ff8  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 ca 02 2d f8 11 00 00 00  ..........-.....
            00008008  01 58 1b 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 04 6b  .X.............k
            00008018  65 79 31 06 76 61 6c 75 65 31                    ey1.value1
            ```

            A reader at offset 32,758 finds 10 bytes left in the block, skips them without looking at them, and reads the header at 32,768: CRC32C `0xf82d02ca`, length 17, type `full`, and the sequence number 7,000, which is `58 1b` followed by six zeros.

            Had 18 bytes been left instead, the writer would have written a `first` fragment there with one byte of payload, and the other 16 bytes of the batch in a `last` fragment at 32,768. A batch is never moved to the next block to avoid a split.
        }

        # Reading a Segment @log-reading
        {
            A reader decodes one segment at a time, keeping four things: its offset in the segment, the end of the last complete batch, the fragments of the batch it is reassembling if any, and the sequence number it expects next. The end of the last complete batch is where the segment is good up to; it starts at 0.

            {[#]
                - At the end of the segment, stop. If a batch is being reassembled, the segment ends inside it: report a bad record of the kind `log.short` at the offset of that batch's `first` fragment. Otherwise the segment ended cleanly.
                - If fewer than 18 bytes are left in the current block, move to the next block boundary and go back to the first step. Padding that reaches the end of the segment is a clean end.
                - If fewer than 17 bytes are left in the segment, report `log.short`: the header itself is cut.
                - Decode the header. If the length is 0, or greater than what is left of the block after the header, report `log.length`.
                - If fewer than `length` bytes follow the header in the segment, report `log.short`: the payload is cut.
                - Compute the CRC32C of the type, the sequence number and the payload, and compare it with the stored one. If they differ, report `log.checksum`.
                - Check the type and the fragment order, as @log-fragmentation gives them. A type outside 1 to 4, or a fragment out of place, is `log.fragment`.
                - When a `full` or a `last` record completes a batch, check its sequence number against the expected one and decode it as @log-batch-encoding says. A gap or an overlap is `log.sequence`, and a payload that does not decode is `log.batch`. Otherwise deliver the batch, set the expected sequence number to `seqno + count`, and set the good end to the end of this record.
                - Move to the end of the record and go back to the first step.
            }

            The first bad record ends the segment for the reader: nothing after it is decoded, delivered or checked. A reader reports the offset of the bad record, the kind of the failure, and the good end. The good end can lie before the bad record: when the bad record is a fragment, or ends a segment in the middle of a batch, the fragments of that batch before it belong to no complete batch and are not good either.

            The same steps in Go, for a reader built beside the `encode` function of `src/wal.go`, with the batch decoding and the reporting left out:

            ```go
            const blockSize, headerSize = 32768, 17

            for off < len(seg) {
                left := blockSize - off%blockSize
                if left < headerSize+1 {
                    off += left // padding
                    continue
                }
                if len(seg)-off < headerSize {
                    return bad(off, "log.short")
                }
                length := int(binary.LittleEndian.Uint32(seg[off+4:]))
                if length == 0 || length > left-headerSize {
                    return bad(off, "log.length")
                }
                end := off + headerSize + length
                if end > len(seg) {
                    return bad(off, "log.short")
                }
                stored := binary.LittleEndian.Uint32(seg[off:])
                if crc32.Checksum(seg[off+8:end], castagnoli) != stored {
                    return bad(off, "log.checksum")
                }
                if err := assemble(seg[off+8], seg[off+9:end], off, end); err != nil {
                    return err // log.fragment, log.sequence or log.batch
                }
                off = end
            }
            return endOfSegment() // log.short if a batch is still open
            ```
        }

        # Torn and Corrupt Tails @log-torn-tail
        {
            A power loss can leave the block being written at the tail of a segment only partly on disk, as [Power Loss and Torn Writes](../architecture.sdoc#torn-writes) describes. On disk, a torn tail looks like one of these, and the reader of @log-reading reports each as shown:

            {[table]
                What is on disk | Reported as
                The segment ends inside a header | `log.short`
                The segment ends inside a payload | `log.short`
                The segment ends after a `first` or a `middle` fragment | `log.short`, at the batch's `first` fragment
                The tail holds the start of a record and then stale or zeroed bytes | `log.checksum`, or `log.length` if the length field is among the stale bytes
                The tail holds zeros where the filesystem extended the file before the data arrived | `log.checksum` or `log.length`: a header of zeros has a length of 0
            }

            A reader cannot tell a torn tail from damage in the middle of a segment by the record itself: a flipped bit in an old record fails its checksum exactly like a torn one. The format therefore has one rule for both. The bad record and everything after it in the segment are treated as never written, and the segment is good up to the good end that @log-reading computed. Records in later segments are not replayed, because the order of visibility would no longer hold.

            What the engine then does with that result is recovery: it truncates the segment at the good end, logs the bytes it discarded, counts the truncation in `tidewater_wal_truncations_total`, and flushes what it replayed before it accepts writes. Those steps, and why they are safe, are in the Recovery part of [Write-Ahead Log](../architecture.sdoc#write-ahead-log); the checkpoint the recovery flush leaves in the manifest, which keeps the segments that were not replayed out of every later replay, is in @recovery-checkpoint. The line the engine logs names the segment, the offset of the bad record and the bytes discarded:

            ```
            level=WARN msg="wal segment truncated" segment=wal/000415.log offset=7351813 discarded_bytes=4127
            ```

            A reader that only inspects, such as `tidewater-admin wal verify`, must not truncate anything. It reports the first bad record of each segment and goes on to the next segment, marking the later ones as not replayed:

            ```
            wal/000520.log   ok    1290331 records
            wal/000521.log   bad record at offset 7351813: checksum mismatch (stored 0x5e1c09a2, computed 0x0b37d4f1)
            wal/000522.log   ok    88213 records, not replayed: follows a bad record
            ```

            # Example: A Torn Tail @log-example-torn
            {
                A segment holding the record of @log-example-full, then a second put, of `key2` to `value2` with sequence number 2, of which only the first 20 of its 34 bytes reached the disk:

                ```
                00000000  fa f0 16 68 11 00 00 00 01 01 00 00 00 00 00 00  ...h............
                00000010  00 01 00 00 00 01 04 6b 65 79 31 06 76 61 6c 75  .......key1.valu
                00000020  65 31 c0 d8 5f d2 11 00 00 00 01 02 00 00 00 00  e1.._...........
                00000030  00 00 00 01 00 00                                ......
                ```

                The first record is whole and good, so the good end is 34. The second header, at offset 34, is complete and declares a payload of 17 bytes, but only 3 follow it before the end of the file. The reader reports `log.short` at offset 34, the segment is truncated to 34 bytes, 20 bytes are discarded, and the database opens with `key1` and without `key2`.

                Had the whole second record reached the disk with one bit of its value flipped, its stored CRC32C, `0xd25fd8c0`, would not have matched the computed `0x0f1a7278`, and the reader would have reported `log.checksum` at offset 34 with the same good end.
            }

            # Example: A Bad Record Before Good Ones @log-example-middle
            {
                A segment of three whole records, puts of `key1`, `key2` and `key3` with the sequence numbers 1, 2 and 3, in which one byte of the second record has gone bad on the disk: byte 22 of the record, the length of its key, reads `00` instead of `04`.

                ```
                00000000  fa f0 16 68 11 00 00 00 01 01 00 00 00 00 00 00  ...h............
                00000010  00 01 00 00 00 01 04 6b 65 79 31 06 76 61 6c 75  .......key1.valu
                00000020  65 31 c0 d8 5f d2 11 00 00 00 01 02 00 00 00 00  e1.._...........
                00000030  00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 00 6b 65 79 32 06 76 61  .........key2.va
                00000040  6c 75 65 32 79 ed 3c 47 11 00 00 00 01 03 00 00  lue2y.<G........
                00000050  00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 04 6b 65 79 33 06  ...........key3.
                00000060  76 61 6c 75 65 33                                value3
                ```

                {[table]
                    Offset | Record | Result
                    0 | Sequence number 1 | Good; the good end becomes 34
                    34 | Sequence number 2, stored CRC32C `0xd25fd8c0`, computed `0x17988800` | `log.checksum` at offset 34
                    68 | Sequence number 3, CRC32C `0x473ced79`, intact | Never read
                }

                The checksum catches the damage before the batch is decoded, so the reader never sees a key of length 0 followed by bytes that do not fit. The third record is intact, but it follows the bad one and is not replayed: the segment is good up to offset 34, and recovery truncates it there, discarding 68 bytes and the write of `key3` with them. `tidewater-admin wal verify`, run on a copy taken before the open, reports it as:

                ```
                wal/000001.log   bad record at offset 34: checksum mismatch (stored 0xd25fd8c0, computed 0x17988800)
                ```

                Damage of this kind, in a record that was whole when written, is not a torn write. When it shows up anywhere but at the tail of the last segment, or after a process crash with no power loss, it is corruption to escalate, as [Truncation](../runbooks/wal-recovery.sdoc#truncation) explains.
            }
        }

        # Validation Rules @log-validation
        {
            Every failure a reader can find in the bytes of a segment is one of six kinds. All six end the segment for the reader in the same way, as @log-torn-tail describes; they differ only in what they report, which is what tells a torn write from a bug.

            {[table]
                Code | Check that failed | Typical cause | Message
                `log.short` | The segment ends inside a header, a payload or a batch | A torn write at the tail | `bad record at offset N: cut short (L payload bytes declared, P present)`
                `log.length` | The length is 0, or runs past the end of its block | A torn or damaged header | `bad record at offset N: length L runs past the end of the block`
                `log.checksum` | The stored CRC32C differs from the computed one | A torn write, or a damaged sector | `bad record at offset N: checksum mismatch (stored 0xS, computed 0xC)`
                `log.fragment` | A type outside 1 to 4, or a fragment out of order, out of place in its block, or with another sequence number than its `first` | A lost fragment, or a writer bug | `bad record at offset N: unexpected middle fragment`
                `log.sequence` | A batch whose sequence number does not follow the previous one, or is 2^56 or more | A segment from another database, or a lost segment | `bad record at offset N: sequence number 7, want 5`
                `log.batch` | A count of 0, an unknown entry kind, a key or value past the payload, or bytes after the last entry | A writer bug | `bad record at offset N: batch entry 2 has unknown kind 9`
            }

            A seventh code is not a failure of the bytes: `log.unreadable` is an error from the filesystem while reading a segment. It is returned as it is, wrapped with the segment's name, and nothing is truncated, since the bytes may well be intact.

            The last three kinds come from records that pass their checksum, which a torn write does not produce. `tidewater-admin wal verify` prints them like the others, and they are always worth escalating, with the segment kept aside, as [the WAL recovery runbook](../runbooks/wal-recovery.sdoc) explains.
        }

        # Writing a Segment @log-writing
        {
            A writer of segments, the engine or a tool that produces test logs, must meet these requirements so that every compatible reader decodes its output:

            {[.]
                - Append only. Bytes once written are never rewritten, except by recovery truncating a bad tail.
                - Lay batches out exactly as @log-fragmentation gives, padding only where fewer than 18 bytes are left in a block.
                - Give every record of a batch the batch's sequence number, and every batch the number that follows the previous batch's last entry.
                - Write a batch's records in a single append, so that a crash can tear only the tail of the last batch written.
                - Never write a record type, entry kind or count that this section does not list.
            }

            A writer need not fsync at any particular point for the format to hold: the format is designed so that whatever prefix of the appends reaches the disk decodes to whole batches up to a good end.
        }
    }

    # Memtable Flush and Table Naming @flush-and-naming
    {
        A flush turns one frozen memtable into one table on level 0. This section specifies the files a flush creates, the names they get and the manifest edit that makes the new table live. A compaction publishes its output tables the same way, so this is also how every table comes into a database.

        When a flush happens, and how many memtables may wait for one, is the engine's business: see [Memtable](../architecture.sdoc#memtable) and [the memtable component](../components/memtable.sdoc). The format only fixes what is on disk before, during and after it.

        # What a Flush Writes @flush-output
        {
            A flush writes exactly one table, on level 0, holding the memtable's entries in internal-key order: the newest version of every key, and every older version a live snapshot may still need. Tombstones are written as entries of kind 0, and puts with an expiry as entries of kind 2 with their expiry, whether or not it has passed: dropping expired values is compaction's job, as [Time to Live](../architecture.sdoc#time-to-live) says, never a flush's.

            {[table]
                Property of the new table | Value after a flush
                Level | 0, recorded in the manifest, not in the table
                Format version | 6, in the footer
                `tidewater.creation-reason` | 1, a flush
                `tidewater.seek-count` | 0
                Sequence numbers | Those of the memtable's entries, so the table's range lies above every older level 0 table's
            }

            A memtable is never empty when it is frozen, and a flush keeps the newest version of every key, so a flushed table always holds at least one entry. A compaction whose output would be empty writes no table at all. A reader may therefore rely on every table holding at least one entry.
        }

        # Allocating the File Number @flush-file-number
        {
            The flush takes the next number from the database's counter of @file-numbers. The number is allocated in memory and becomes durable only with the manifest edit that records the table, which carries `next-file-number` one higher than it, or higher still when other files were allocated meanwhile.

            By then the memtable's own segment has a lower number, since freezing the memtable opened the next segment, and the table a higher one. In the example used throughout this document, the frozen memtable's records are in `wal/000949.log`, freezing it opened `wal/000950.log`, and the flush writes `sst/000951.sst`.
        }

        # Publishing the Table @flush-publish
        {
            {[#]
                - Write the whole table to `sst/NNNNNN.sst.tmp`: the data blocks in key order, then the filter block, the properties block, the index block and the footer.
                - Fsync the file.
                - Rename it to `sst/NNNNNN.sst`.
                - Fsync the `sst/` directory, so that the rename survives a power loss.
                - Append the edit of @flush-edit to the manifest and fsync it, as [Writing an Edit](../components/manifest.sdoc#edit-write) describes.
            }

            Until the last step the table is not part of the database, whatever its name: a crash at any point before it leaves an orphan, removed on the next open as @orphans describes. A reader must never open a `.tmp` file as a table, and must never treat a table that no live edit names as part of the database, even if it is complete and passes every check.

            A table is never modified after the rename. Every later change to the set of live tables is a manifest edit, and a table's bytes stay what the flush or compaction wrote until the file is deleted.
        }

        # Recording the Table @flush-edit
        {
            The edit that records a flush holds these fields, encoded as @manifest-fields specifies:

            {[table]
                Field | Value
                `add-table` | Level 0, the table's file number, its size in bytes, its smallest and largest internal keys, and its smallest and largest sequence numbers
                `log-number` | The number of the oldest segment still holding records that are in no table: the segment of the oldest memtable still waiting for a flush, or the active segment
                `next-file-number` | One more than the highest file number allocated so far
                `last-sequence` | The highest sequence number in the new table, if higher than the last one recorded
            }

            The byte-for-byte encoding of such an edit, for `sst/000951.sst`, is in @manifest-example. A flush forced by a checkpoint is followed by a second edit of its own, holding `checkpoint`, as @checkpoint-records describes.
        }

        # Deleting Flushed Segments @flush-segment-deletion
        {
            Once the edit is durable, every segment numbered below its `log-number` holds only records that are in tables, and the engine deletes those segments. A segment is never deleted before the edit that moves `log-number` past it is fsynced, so that a crash between the two leaves the segment for replay, where its records are skipped as @replay-start explains.

            A segment numbered below `log-number` that is still present on open is obsolete: a crash came between the edit and the deletion. Open deletes it without reading it.
        }

        # Compaction Output @compaction-output
        {
            A compaction writes each of its output tables exactly as a flush writes its one table, through a temporary name, an fsync and a rename, with `tidewater.creation-reason` set to 2. It then records the whole compaction in a single edit: a `delete-table` for every input and an `add-table` for every output, so that no version ever holds both the inputs and the outputs, or neither.

            Output tables are always written in the newest format version, whatever the versions of their inputs, which is how a database moves to a new format, as @lazy-upgrade explains. The manifest records the level each output goes to, and nothing in the table depends on it: a table's bytes are the same on whatever level it lives.
        }

        # Orphans @orphans
        {
            On open, after the manifest has been replayed, the engine deletes the files that a crash left behind and that the database no longer needs. Each kind is recognised by its name alone:

            {[table]
                File | Deleted when
                `sst/NNNNNN.sst.tmp` | Always: a table that was never published
                `sst/NNNNNN.sst` | No table of the current version has its number
                `MANIFEST-NNNNNN` | `CURRENT` names another manifest
                `CURRENT.tmp` | Always: a pointer that was never installed
                `wal/NNNNNN.log` | Its number is below the manifest's `log-number`
            }

            Files whose names match none of the patterns of @database-files are not orphans and are never deleted. `tidewater-admin gc DIR` deletes the same files open would, on a closed database, and refuses to run while `LOCK` is held.

            The rule is safe only because file numbers are never reused, as @file-numbers explains: a table that a crash orphaned can never be mistaken for a later, live table of the same number.
        }
    }

    # SSTable Format @sstable-format
    {
        An SSTable is an immutable file of sorted entries in blocks, followed by a filter block, a properties block, an index block and a fixed-size footer. Its parts, and how the engine uses each one, are summarised in [SSTables](../components/sstables.sdoc); this section gives their bytes.

        # Layout @sstable-layout
        {
            ```
            +------------+-----+------------+--------+------------+--------+--------+
            | data block | ... | data block | filter | properties | index  | footer |
            | + trailer  |     | + trailer  | block  | block      | block  |        |
            |            |     |            | + trl. | + trailer  | + trl. |        |
            +------------+-----+------------+--------+------------+--------+--------+
                                                                    last 60 bytes ^
            ```

            {[table]
                Part | Count | Encoding | Found through
                Data block | One or more | @data-blocks | The index block
                Filter block | Exactly one | @filter-block | The footer
                Properties block | Exactly one | @properties-block | The footer
                Index block | Exactly one | @index-block | The footer
                Footer | Exactly one, the last 60 bytes | @sstable-footer | The end of the file
            }

            Every block is followed by a trailer, as @block-trailer gives it. A writer writes the parts in the order above, with no gaps between them. A reader must not rely on the order or on the absence of gaps: it finds every block through a handle, and a table whose parts were laid out differently but whose handles are right reads the same way.

            A table holds at least one data block, and every data block holds at least one entry. There is no table header: the first byte of a table is the first byte of its first data block.
        }

        # Block Handles @block-handles
        {
            A block handle locates one block: its offset from the start of the file and its size in bytes, not counting the trailer that follows it.

            {[table]
                Where | Encoding of the offset and the size
                In the footer | Two `uint64`
                In an index block entry's value | Two varints, offset first
            }

            A handle is valid when its size is not 0 and the block and its trailer end at or before the start of the footer. A handle that is not valid makes the table corrupt, with the code `sst.handle`, before the reader reads anything through it. Whether the block is big enough for its kind, at least 8 bytes once decompressed for a block with a restart array, is checked when the block is decoded.

            Handles of different blocks must not overlap. A reader need not check this for data blocks, which it reaches one at a time, but must check it for the three blocks the footer names.
        }

        # Block Trailer @block-trailer
        {
            In format versions 4 to 6, every block, of whatever kind, is followed by a trailer of 5 bytes:

            {[table]
                Offset after the block | Size | Field | Encoding | Meaning
                0 | 1 | compression | `uint8` | How the block's bytes are stored, as in @compression
                1 | 4 | checksum | `uint32` | CRC32C of the block's bytes as stored, followed by the compression byte
            }

            The checksum is computed over the stored bytes, compressed if the block is compressed, and then over the one compression byte, as if they were contiguous, which they are. A reader verifies it before it decompresses or decodes anything, so that a damaged block can never reach the decompressor or the decoder. The block cache does the same on every miss, as [the block cache component](../components/block-cache.sdoc) says: a block that fails is never inserted.

            In format version 3, which predates compression, the trailer is the 4-byte CRC32C of the block's bytes alone, with no compression byte. A reader takes the trailer's size from the format version in the footer: 4 bytes in version 3, 5 bytes from version 4 on.

            A mismatch makes the read fail with `ErrCorrupt` for the block's key range and increments `tidewater_block_checksum_failures_total`, as [SSTables](../architecture.sdoc#sstables) describes; the table is then marked suspect in the manifest, as @manifest-fields shows. The damaged bytes are never returned.
        }

        # Compression @compression
        {
            {[table]
                Value | Name | Allowed in | Since format
                0 | none | Every block | 4, and implied in 3
                1 | Snappy | Data blocks only | 4
                2 to 255 | none | Nowhere: a reader treats the block as corrupt |
            }

            Only data blocks are ever compressed. Filter blocks are close to random and would not shrink, and index and properties blocks are read once, when the table is opened, so compressing them would save little and cost a decompression on every open. A compression byte of 1 on a filter, properties or index block makes the table corrupt, with the code `sst.compression`, and so does any byte other than 0 or 1.

            Snappy here is the Snappy block format, the one `snappy.Encode` writes in Go, not the framed stream format: the stored bytes start with the uncompressed length as a varint, followed by Snappy's literal and copy elements, with no stream identifier and no per-chunk checksums. The block's own CRC32C protects it instead.

            A writer compresses a data block with Snappy and keeps the compressed form only if it shrinks the block by at least an eighth: with `raw` the uncompressed size and `c` the compressed size, it stores the compressed form when `c <= raw - raw/8`, in integer arithmetic, and the raw block with compression 0 otherwise.

            {[table]
                Uncompressed size | Largest compressed size kept
                16,384 | 14,336
                4,096 | 3,584
                100 | 88
            }

            A reader decompresses a block with compression 1 after verifying its checksum, and treats as corrupt, with the code `sst.snappy`, a block whose decoding fails or whose decoded length differs from the length its preamble declares. The 16 bytes `abababababababab` compress to these 7 bytes, a 2-byte literal followed by a 14-byte copy at offset 2:

            ```
            10 04 61 62 36 02 00
            ```
        }

        # Internal Keys @internal-keys
        {
            Every key in a data block and in an index block is an internal key: the user key followed by an 8-byte trailer, the `uint64` `(seqno << 8) | kind`, little-endian.

            ```
            user:1017 at sequence number 1,284,717, kind 2:
            75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 31 37   02 6d 9a 13 00 00 00 00
            u  s  e  r  :  1  0  1  7    (1284717 << 8) | 2 = 0x139a6d02
            ```

            The trailer packs the sequence number into its upper 56 bits, which is why a sequence number must stay below 2^56 everywhere, in the log as well, as @log-sequence-numbers says. The kind takes the low byte, which comes first on disk: the first byte of the trailer is the kind, and the next seven are the sequence number.

            Internal keys sort by user key ascending, compared bytewise as `bytes.Compare` does, then by sequence number descending, so that the newest version of a key comes first. The kind is not part of the order: no two entries of a table share a sequence number, so two internal keys never differ by their kind alone. A comparator that compares whole trailers as integers, descending, gives the same order and is what the engine uses.

            An internal key shorter than 8 bytes is corrupt, with the code `sst.key`: it has no room for its trailer. A user key may be empty, so an internal key of exactly 8 bytes is valid.

            # Entry Kinds @entry-kinds
            {
                The kind of an internal key says what the entry's value is. The same values are used for the entries of a batch in the log, as @log-batch-encoding shows.

                {[table]
                    Kind | Name | Value in a data block | Since format
                    0 | delete | Empty: a tombstone | 3
                    1 | put | The user value | 3
                    2 | put with expiry | The expiry as a `uint64`, then the user value | 6
                    3 to 254 | none | Reserved: a table holding one is corrupt |
                    255 | none | Never stored: used only in lookup keys, as @block-search explains |
                }

                A kind-0 entry with a non-empty value, a kind-2 entry whose value is shorter than 8 bytes, and a kind-2 entry in a table of format version 3 to 5 are all corrupt, with the code `sst.key`.
            }
        }

        # Data Blocks @data-blocks
        {
            A data block holds a run of entries in internal-key order, then the offsets of its restart points, then their number. Its uncompressed size is set by the writer, which closes a block once its entries reach 16 KiB; an entry larger than that makes a block of its own, as large as the entry. A reader does not depend on the size, and must accept a block of any size its handle gives.

            ```
            +---------+---------+-----+---------+-----------+-----+-----------+-----------+
            | entry 0 | entry 1 | ... | entry n | restart 0 | ... | restart m | m + 1     |
            |         |         |     |         | uint32    |     | uint32    | uint32    |
            +---------+---------+-----+---------+-----------+-----+-----------+-----------+
            ```

            The keys of a table are strictly ascending in internal-key order across the whole table: within a block, and from the last key of one block to the first key of the next. A reader that finds a key not greater than the one before it reports the table corrupt, with the code `sst.order`.

            # Entry Encoding @data-block-entries
            {
                Each entry stores its key as a suffix of the previous entry's key, so that keys sharing a prefix, as sorted keys mostly do, are stored once:

                {[table]
                    Field | Encoding | Meaning
                    shared | varint | Bytes at the start of the key that are the same as the previous entry's key
                    unshared | varint | Bytes of the key that follow them
                    value length | varint | Bytes of the value
                    key suffix | `unshared` bytes | The key, from byte `shared` on
                    value | `value length` bytes | The value, as @entry-kinds gives it for the entry's kind
                }

                The full key is the first `shared` bytes of the previous entry's full key, followed by the suffix. `shared` is 0 for the first entry of a block and at every restart point. A `shared` greater than the previous key's length, or a suffix or value that runs into the restart array, makes the block corrupt, with the code `sst.block`.

                The prefix is shared over the whole internal key, trailer included. Two versions of one user key share their whole user key and differ in the trailer, so the second costs 3 bytes of lengths, 8 of trailer and its value.
            }

            # Restart Points @restart-points
            {
                Every sixteenth entry of a data block, counting from the first, is a restart point: it is stored with `shared` set to 0, so it can be decoded without the entries before it. The block ends with the offsets of its restart points, each a `uint32` counted from the start of the block, in ascending order, followed by their number as a `uint32`.

                {[table]
                    Block | Restart interval
                    Data block | 16 entries
                    Properties block | 16 entries
                    Index block | 1 entry: every entry is a restart point
                }

                The interval is a writer's choice and is not stored. A reader uses only the restart array, and must accept any interval, including blocks written with different intervals in one table.

                A reader checks the array before it uses it: the number of restart points is at least 1, the array fits in the block, the first offset is 0, the offsets ascend, and every offset lies before the start of the array. A block that fails any of these is corrupt, with the code `sst.block`.
            }

            # Searching a Block @block-search
            {
                A point read looks for the newest version of a user key at or below the read's sequence number. It builds a lookup key: the user key followed by the trailer `(seqno << 8) | 255`, which sorts before every entry of that user key with a sequence number at or below the read's, and after every entry with a higher one.

                {[#]
                    - Binary-search the restart points for the last one whose key is less than the lookup key, or take the first one if none is. Restart points are decoded without context, so each probe of the search costs one entry.
                    - From there, decode entries forward until one is greater than or equal to the lookup key, or the restart array is reached.
                    - If the entry found has the read's user key, it is the answer: a put returns its value, a delete returns absent, and a put with expiry returns its value unless its expiry has passed.
                    - Otherwise the block holds no visible version of the key.
                }

                For `user:1017` read at sequence number 1,284,720, the lookup key is:

                ```
                75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 31 37   ff 70 9a 13 00 00 00 00
                ```

                The entry for `user:1017` at 1,284,717 is the first that is not less than it, so the read returns that entry's value. Read at 1,284,716, the lookup key would sort after that entry, and the scan would go on to `user:1100`, a different user key: no visible version.
            }

            # Values with an Expiry @expiring-values
            {
                In format version 6, a put with an expiry is stored as an entry of kind 2 whose value starts with the expiry time, a `uint64` of milliseconds since the Unix epoch, followed by the user value:

                ```
                00 38 d3 60 ba 01 00 00   62 6f 62
                expiry 1,900,000,000,000  "bob"
                ```

                A reader that returns the value strips the 8 bytes. Whether an entry has expired is decided by comparing its expiry with the wall clock of the node serving the read, as [Time to Live](../architecture.sdoc#time-to-live) describes: an entry whose expiry is at or before the current time reads as absent. The format stores the time and nothing else; it does not record which clock wrote it.

                Expired entries stay in their table until compaction rewrites it. A reader must not treat an expired entry as damage, and must not skip it when it answers whether a key has any visible version: an expired put hides older versions of its key exactly as a tombstone does.
            }

            # Example: A Data Block @data-block-example
            {
                The data block of the table used throughout this document holds four entries, all on one restart point. It is 88 bytes, followed by its 5-byte trailer, at offset 0 of `sst/000951.sst`:

                ```
                00000000  00 11 05 75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 35 01 61 9a 13  ...user:1005.a..
                00000010  00 00 00 00 61 6c 69 63 65 08 09 00 36 00 62 9a  ....alice...6.b.
                00000020  13 00 00 00 00 07 0a 0b 31 37 02 6d 9a 13 00 00  ........17.m....
                00000030  00 00 00 38 d3 60 ba 01 00 00 62 6f 62 06 0b 05  ...8.`....bob...
                00000040  31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00 63 61 72 6f 6c  100.}......carol
                00000050  00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 4f d0 c5 8f           .........O...
                ```

                {[table]
                    Offset | Lengths | Key suffix | Entry
                    0 | `00 11 05`: shared 0, unshared 17, value 5 | `user:1005` and trailer `01 61 9a 13 00 00 00 00` | Put of `alice` at 1,284,705
                    25 | `08 09 00`: shared 8, unshared 9, value 0 | `6` and trailer `00 62 9a 13 00 00 00 00` | Delete of `user:1006` at 1,284,706
                    37 | `07 0a 0b`: shared 7, unshared 10, value 11 | `17` and trailer `02 6d 9a 13 00 00 00 00` | Put of `bob` at 1,284,717, expiring at 1,900,000,000,000
                    61 | `06 0b 05`: shared 6, unshared 11, value 5 | `100` and trailer `01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00` | Put of `carol` at 1,284,733
                    80 | | | Restart array: one restart point, at offset 0
                    84 | | | Number of restart points: 1
                    88 | | | Trailer: compression 0, CRC32C `0x8fc5d04f`
                }

                The second key shares `user:100` with the first, the third `user:10` with the second, and the fourth `user:1` with the third.
            }

            # Example: A Compressed Data Block @compressed-block-example
            {
                Four puts, of `event:0001` to `event:0004` at the sequence numbers 2,001,001 to 2,001,004, each with the 36-byte value `{"status":"ok","region":"eu-west-1"}`, make a data block of 209 bytes. Snappy compresses it to 92 bytes, well under the 183 that saving an eighth allows, so the writer stores the compressed form and sets the compression byte to 1. The stored block and its trailer:

                ```
                00000000  d1 01 ec 00 12 24 65 76 65 6e 74 3a 30 30 30 31  .....$event:0001
                00000010  01 69 88 1e 00 00 00 00 7b 22 73 74 61 74 75 73  .i......{"status
                00000020  22 3a 22 6f 6b 22 2c 22 72 65 67 69 6f 6e 22 3a  ":"ok","region":
                00000030  22 65 75 2d 77 65 73 74 2d 31 22 7d 09 09 24 08  "eu-west-1"}..$.
                00000040  32 01 6a b2 30 00 08 33 01 6b b2 60 00 08 34 01  2.j.0..3.k.`..4.
                00000050  6c a6 90 00 0e b8 00 0c 01 00 00 00 01 d8 6e 01  l.............n.
                00000060  e9                                               .
                ```

                {[table]
                    Offset | Bytes | Snappy element | What it rebuilds
                    0 | `d1 01` | Preamble | The block is 209 bytes once decompressed
                    2 | `ec` and 60 bytes | Literal of 60 bytes | The first entry, 57 bytes, and the second entry's lengths, `09 09 24`
                    63 | `08` and `32 01 6a` | Literal of 3 bytes | The second key's suffix, `2`, and the first two bytes of its trailer
                    67 | `b2 30 00` | Copy of 45 bytes from 48 back | The rest of the trailer, the value, and the third entry's lengths
                    70 | `08` and `33 01 6b` | Literal of 3 bytes | The third key's suffix and the start of its trailer
                    74 | `b2 60 00` | Copy of 45 bytes from 96 back | As at 67, for the third entry
                    77 | `08` and `34 01 6c` | Literal of 3 bytes | The fourth key's suffix and the start of its trailer
                    81 | `a6 90 00` | Copy of 42 bytes from 144 back | The rest of the fourth trailer and its value
                    84 | `0e b8 00` | Copy of 4 bytes from 184 back | The restart offset, four zero bytes
                    87 | `0c` and `01 00 00 00` | Literal of 4 bytes | The restart count, 1
                    92 | `01 d8 6e 01 e9` | Trailer | Compression 1, and the CRC32C `0xe9016ed8` of the 92 stored bytes followed by `01`
                }

                The reader verifies the CRC32C over the 92 stored bytes and the compression byte, decompresses, checks that the result is 209 bytes long as the preamble says, and only then decodes the entries. Any copy may reach back to any earlier byte of the block, as the copy at offset 74 does: a decoder must not assume the shortest distance.
            }
        }

        # Filter Block @filter-block
        {
            Every table has exactly one filter block, holding one bloom filter over every distinct user key in the table. Filters per data block were proposed and rejected in [D-0012](../decisions.sdoc#d-0012), and the reasons are set out in [Bloom Filters](../components/bloom-filters.sdoc#filter-per-table). The layout below has not changed since format version 3, the oldest the engine reads.

            ```
            +----------------------------------+--------+---------+
            | bit array                        | probes | trailer |
            | ceil(keys x bits per key / 8) B  | uint8  |         |
            +----------------------------------+--------+---------+
            ```

            {[table]
                Offset | Size | Field | Encoding | Meaning
                0 | `size − 1` | bits | bytes | The filter's bit array; bit `b` is the bit of value `1 << (b % 8)` in byte `b / 8`
                `size − 1` | 1 | probes | `uint8` | The number of probes per key, from 1 to 30
            }

            The filter block is never compressed: its trailer's compression byte is always 0. A filter block of fewer than 2 bytes, or whose probe count is 0 or more than 30, makes the table corrupt, with the code `sst.filter`.

            # Building the Filter @filter-building
            {
                The writer builds the filter from the user keys of the table's entries, in order, skipping a key equal to the one before it, so that the versions of one key count once. With `n` the number of distinct user keys and `bpk` the bits per key the table is written with:

                {[.]
                    - The bit array has `ceil(n × bpk / 8)` bytes, and its size in bits, `m`, is eight times that.
                    - The number of probes is `bpk × ln 2`, rounded to the nearest integer and kept between 1 and 30.
                    - Each key is hashed once with xxh3-64, as @xxh3-64 defines it. Its low 32 bits are `lo` and its high 32 bits are `hi`.
                    - Probe `i`, for `i` from 0 to the number of probes minus one, sets bit `(lo + i × hi) mod m`, computed on integers wide enough not to overflow: 64 bits are plenty, since `lo + 29 × hi` is below 2^37.
                }

                The bits per key are the value of the `BloomBitsPerKey` option when the table was written, recorded in the table's `tidewater.bloom-bits-per-key` property. The default value, and the false-positive rate it gives, are in [Bloom Filters](../architecture.sdoc#bloom-filters); this document does not repeat them, and a reader never needs them, since the probe count is stored in the block. Tables written under different settings therefore coexist in one database.
            }

            # Querying the Filter @filter-query
            {
                A key may be in the table if and only if all of its probe bits are set. A reader hashes the user key, computes its probe positions with the table's own `m` and probe count, and stops at the first clear bit, which proves the key absent from the table. A filter never proves a key present.

                Range reads do not consult the filter, since a range can match keys the filter was never asked about. Tombstones and expired entries are in the filter like any other key: a read of a deleted key must reach the tombstone that hides the older versions below it.

                When `hi` is a multiple of `m`, every probe of a key lands on the same bit, and the key is tested on one bit instead of all of them. The scheme has done this since format 3, and changing it would change the meaning of every filter on disk. The effect is a false positive more often for the few keys concerned, one reason the measured rates stay close to, rather than at, the theoretical ones in [Bloom Filters](../components/bloom-filters.sdoc#filter-memory).
            }

            # Example: A Filter Block @filter-example
            {
                The table of @data-block-example has four distinct user keys. Written with `BloomBitsPerKey` set to 12, which is not the default, its filter has `ceil(4 × 12 / 8)` = 6 bytes, so `m` = 48 bits, and `12 × ln 2` = 8.32 rounds to 8 probes.

                {[table]
                    User key | xxh3-64 | lo | hi | Bits set
                    `user:1005` | `0xfadb677fa84a8351` | `0xa84a8351` | `0xfadb677f` | 1, 16, 31, 46, 13, 28, 43, 10
                    `user:1006` | `0x9c6bea57cb516270` | `0xcb516270` | `0x9c6bea57` | 32, 7, 30, 5, 28, 3, 26, 1
                    `user:1017` | `0xb83b9a8240095785` | `0x40095785` | `0xb83b9a82` | 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19
                    `user:1100` | `0xafe92d5bfa7f3518` | `0xfa7f3518` | `0xafe92d5b` | 40, 35, 30, 25, 20, 15, 10, 5
                }

                Bits 1, 3, 5 and 7 are set in byte 0, which is therefore `0xaa`. The block, at offset 93 of the table, with its trailer:

                ```
                0000005d  aa ae 1b d6 09 49 08 00 0e 29 f4 a8              .....I...)..
                ```

                {[table]
                    Bytes | Meaning
                    `aa ae 1b d6 09 49` | The 48 bits
                    `08` | 8 probes
                    `00` | Compression: none
                    `0e 29 f4 a8` | CRC32C `0xa8f4290e`
                }

                Queries for keys the table does not hold:

                {[table]
                    User key | xxh3-64 | Probe positions | Answer
                    `user:1003` | `0x1d63c3a640006617` | 39, 45, 3, 9, 15, 21, 27, 33 | Absent: bit 39 is clear
                    `user:3001` | `0x7dfb81ba6f3d29f8` | 8, 18, 28, 38, 0, 10, 20, 30 | Absent: bit 8 is clear
                    `user:1002` | `0xae0030009279872b` | 43, eight times | May be present: a false positive
                }

                The high half of the hash of `user:1002`, `0xae003000`, is a multiple of 48, so all eight of its probes test bit 43, which `user:1005` happens to set. The read then searches the index and the data block, and finds nothing.
            }
        }

        # Properties Block @properties-block
        {
            The properties block describes the table's contents, so that the engine and the tools can choose and report tables without reading their data. It has the encoding of a data block, with a restart interval of 16, but its keys are property names, plain UTF-8 strings with no trailer, in ascending bytewise order, and every value is a single varint. It is never compressed.

            # Required Properties @required-properties
            {
                A table of a given format version must carry every property listed here with that version or an earlier one in its "Since" column. A reader that finds one missing treats the table as corrupt, with the code `sst.property`.

                {[table]
                    Name | Since | Meaning
                    `tidewater.num-entries` | 3 | Entries in the table, of every kind
                    `tidewater.num-deletions` | 3 | Entries of kind 0
                    `tidewater.raw-key-size` | 3 | Total bytes of the internal keys, trailers included, before prefix compression
                    `tidewater.raw-value-size` | 3 | Total bytes of the values as stored in entries, expiry times included, before block compression
                    `tidewater.smallest-seqno` | 3 | Lowest sequence number of any entry
                    `tidewater.largest-seqno` | 3 | Highest sequence number of any entry
                    `tidewater.seek-count` | 5 | Seeks charged to the table when it was written, as @seek-count-property explains
                    `tidewater.tombstone-share` | 5 | Share of the table's distinct user keys whose newest version in the table is a deletion, in parts per 10,000
                    `tidewater.num-expiring` | 6 | Entries of kind 2
                    `tidewater.earliest-expiry` | 6 | The earliest expiry time of any entry of kind 2, in milliseconds since the Unix epoch; absent when `tidewater.num-expiring` is 0
                }

                The values must be consistent with each other: `tidewater.num-deletions` and `tidewater.num-expiring` are at most `tidewater.num-entries`, the smallest sequence number is at most the largest, and the tombstone share is at most 10,000. A reader should check these, and must refuse a table whose sequence numbers in the properties disagree with the ones its manifest edit recorded.
            }

            # Informational Properties @informational-properties
            {
                These are written by the current release for tools and reports. A table may lack any of them, and a reader must not depend on them.

                {[table]
                    Name | Meaning
                    `tidewater.bloom-bits-per-key` | The bits per key the filter block was built with
                    `tidewater.creation-reason` | 1 for a flush, 2 for a compaction, 3 for a table rewritten by `tidewater-admin downgrade`
                    `tidewater.data-size` | Total bytes of the data blocks, trailers included
                    `tidewater.num-data-blocks` | Number of data blocks, equal to the number of index entries
                }

                Properties with any other name are ignored. A reader must accept a table that carries names it does not know, so that a later release can add properties without a new format version, as long as no reader needs them.
            }

            # Seek Count @seek-count-property
            {
                A read that passes a table's filter but finds no entry for its key is a seek against the table, and a table that collects too many is compacted early, by the seek trigger of compaction. The counts collected at run time live in memory, since a table is never modified. `tidewater.seek-count` is the count a table starts with:

                {[.]
                    - A flush writes 0.
                    - A compaction writes, in each output table, the seeks its input tables had collected when the compaction started, shared among the outputs in proportion to the bytes each received, so that a key range that keeps attracting reads for absent keys keeps its count when it moves down a level.
                }

                The counts collected after a table was written are not saved anywhere, and an open starts every table again from its property. Tables of format 3 and 4 have no `tidewater.seek-count`, and the seek trigger never fires for them, as [Table Properties](../components/sstables.sdoc#sstable-properties) says.
            }

            # Tombstone Share @tombstone-share-property
            {
                `tidewater.tombstone-share` counts user keys, not entries. Of the distinct user keys in the table, it is the share whose newest version is a deletion, the keys a scan has to step over, in parts per 10,000, rounded down. Two versions of a key, a put and a later delete, count as one key whose newest version is a deletion.

                It is not derivable from `tidewater.num-deletions`, which counts entries of every version, and that is why tables of format 3 and 4, which lack the property, never trigger the tombstone trigger. Expired values are not counted in it: they expire after the table is written. The engine adds them at run time from `tidewater.num-expiring` and `tidewater.earliest-expiry`, which is what those two properties of format 6 are for.
            }

            # Example: A Properties Block @properties-example
            {
                The properties block of the example table is 251 bytes at offset 105, with fourteen properties on a single restart point:

                {[table]
                    Name | Value bytes | Value
                    `tidewater.bloom-bits-per-key` | `0c` | 12
                    `tidewater.creation-reason` | `01` | Flush
                    `tidewater.data-size` | `5d` | 93: the 88-byte data block and its 5-byte trailer
                    `tidewater.earliest-expiry` | `80 f0 cc 86 a6 37` | 1,900,000,000,000
                    `tidewater.largest-seqno` | `fd b4 4e` | 1,284,733
                    `tidewater.num-data-blocks` | `01` | 1
                    `tidewater.num-deletions` | `01` | 1
                    `tidewater.num-entries` | `04` | 4
                    `tidewater.num-expiring` | `01` | 1
                    `tidewater.raw-key-size` | `44` | 68: four internal keys of 17 bytes
                    `tidewater.raw-value-size` | `15` | 21: 5 + 0 + 11 + 5
                    `tidewater.seek-count` | `00` | 0, as for every flushed table
                    `tidewater.smallest-seqno` | `e1 b4 4e` | 1,284,705
                    `tidewater.tombstone-share` | `c4 13` | 2,500: one of four keys, `user:1006`, is deleted
                }

                ```
                00000069  00 1c 01 74 69 64 65 77 61 74 65 72 2e 62 6c 6f  ...tidewater.blo
                00000079  6f 6d 2d 62 69 74 73 2d 70 65 72 2d 6b 65 79 0c  om-bits-per-key.
                00000089  0a 0f 01 63 72 65 61 74 69 6f 6e 2d 72 65 61 73  ...creation-reas
                00000099  6f 6e 01 0a 09 01 64 61 74 61 2d 73 69 7a 65 5d  on....data-size]
                000000a9  0a 0f 06 65 61 72 6c 69 65 73 74 2d 65 78 70 69  ...earliest-expi
                000000b9  72 79 80 f0 cc 86 a6 37 0a 0d 03 6c 61 72 67 65  ry.....7...large
                000000c9  73 74 2d 73 65 71 6e 6f fd b4 4e 0a 0f 01 6e 75  st-seqno..N...nu
                000000d9  6d 2d 64 61 74 61 2d 62 6c 6f 63 6b 73 01 0f 08  m-data-blocks...
                000000e9  01 65 6c 65 74 69 6f 6e 73 01 0e 07 01 65 6e 74  .eletions....ent
                000000f9  72 69 65 73 04 0f 07 01 78 70 69 72 69 6e 67 01  ries....xpiring.
                00000109  0a 0c 01 72 61 77 2d 6b 65 79 2d 73 69 7a 65 44  ...raw-key-sizeD
                00000119  0e 0a 01 76 61 6c 75 65 2d 73 69 7a 65 15 0a 0a  ...value-size...
                00000129  01 73 65 65 6b 2d 63 6f 75 6e 74 00 0b 0d 03 6d  .seek-count....m
                00000139  61 6c 6c 65 73 74 2d 73 65 71 6e 6f e1 b4 4e 0a  allest-seqno..N.
                00000149  0f 02 74 6f 6d 62 73 74 6f 6e 65 2d 73 68 61 72  ..tombstone-shar
                00000159  65 c4 13 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 4a fd 37 68  e...........J.7h
                ```

                Every name after the first shares at least `tidewater.`, ten bytes, with the one before it: the second entry starts `0a 0f 01`, shared 10, unshared 15, value 1. The block ends with its single restart offset, `00 00 00 00`, the count `01 00 00 00`, and the trailer, compression `00` and CRC32C `0x6837fd4a`.
            }
        }

        # Index Block @index-block
        {
            The index block has one entry for every data block, in the order of the data blocks. It has the encoding of a data block with a restart interval of 1, so every entry is a restart point, and it is never compressed.

            {[table]
                Part of the entry | Content
                Key | A separator: an internal key greater than or equal to the last key of its data block, and less than the first key of the next data block
                Value | The data block's handle: its offset and its size, as two varints, the size not counting the trailer
            }

            The current writer uses the last key of each data block as its separator, which meets the rule trivially. A reader must accept any separator that meets it, including shorter keys an earlier or later writer might choose.

            To find the data block that may hold a lookup key, a reader binary-searches the index for the first separator greater than or equal to it. That block is the only one that can hold a visible version of the key; if no separator qualifies, the key sorts after everything in the table.

            The separators must ascend strictly, and the handles must be valid, as @block-handles defines it. A reader checks the separators when it opens the table and pins the index, and checks each handle when it is first used.

            # Example: An Index Block @index-example
            {
                The example table has one data block, so its index has one entry: the last key of the block, `user:1100` at 1,284,733, and the handle of the block at offset 0, 88 bytes long.

                ```
                00000169  00 11 02 75 73 65 72 3a 31 31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13  ...user:1100.}..
                00000179  00 00 00 00 00 58 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 05  .....X..........
                00000189  a2 5c 7b                                         .\{
                ```

                The entry is `00 11 02`, shared 0, unshared 17, value 2, then the 17-byte key and the handle `00 58`: offset 0, size 88. The restart array, `00 00 00 00` and `01 00 00 00`, and the trailer, `00` and CRC32C `0x7b5ca205`, follow.
            }

            # Example: An Index of Two Blocks @index-two-blocks-example
            {
                A table whose entries for `user:1005` and `user:1006` fill one data block of 4,021 bytes, and whose entries up to `user:1100` fill a second of 3,988 bytes, has an index of two entries. The second block starts at 4,026, after the first block's 5-byte trailer:

                ```
                00000000  00 11 03 75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 36 00 62 9a 13  ...user:1006.b..
                00000010  00 00 00 00 00 b5 1f 00 11 04 75 73 65 72 3a 31  ..........user:1
                00000020  31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00 ba 1f 94 1f 00  100.}...........
                00000030  00 00 00 17 00 00 00 02 00 00 00                 ...........
                ```

                {[table]
                    Entry | Separator | Handle
                    At offset 0 | `user:1006` at 1,284,706, delete: the last key of the first block | `00 b5 1f`: offset 0, size 4,021
                    At offset 23 | `user:1100` at 1,284,733, put: the last key of the second block | `ba 1f 94 1f`: offset 4,026, size 3,988
                }

                Both entries are restart points, with `shared` 0, even though the two keys share `user:1`: an index block's restart interval is 1. The restart array holds 0 and 23, `00 00 00 00 17 00 00 00`, and the count is 2.

                A read of `user:1050` looks for the first separator not less than its lookup key. `user:1006` is less, `user:1100` is not, so only the second block can hold the key. A read of `user:1006` at a sequence number below 1,284,706 builds a lookup key that sorts after the first separator, and goes to the second block as well, where it finds the key's older versions, if the table has any, at the start of the block.
            }
        }

        # Footer @sstable-footer
        {
            The footer is the last 60 bytes of every table. Its layout is the same in every format version from 3 to 6, which is what lets a reader find the version before it knows how to read anything else.

            {[table]
                Offset | Size | Field | Encoding | Meaning
                0 | 8 | index offset | `uint64` | Offset of the index block
                8 | 8 | index size | `uint64` | Size of the index block, without its trailer
                16 | 8 | filter offset | `uint64` | Offset of the filter block
                24 | 8 | filter size | `uint64` | Size of the filter block, without its trailer
                32 | 8 | properties offset | `uint64` | Offset of the properties block
                40 | 8 | properties size | `uint64` | Size of the properties block, without its trailer
                48 | 4 | format version | `uint32` | The format version the table was written in: 6 for every table the current release writes
                52 | 8 | magic | bytes | `54 49 44 45 2d 53 53 54`, the ASCII `TIDE-SST`
            }

            The magic is the constant `tableMagic` in `src/format.go`. Read as a `uint64`, it is `0x5453532d45444954`; a reader may compare either the bytes or the integer, and the two are the same check.

            The footer carries no checksum. It is checked by its magic, by its version, and by the validity of its three handles, and every block it points to carries its own CRC32C, so a damaged footer is caught by one of those checks or by the first block read through it.

            A reader reads and checks the footer in this order, and stops at the first failure:

            {[#]
                - The file is at least 60 bytes long, or the table is corrupt with the code `sst.size`.
                - The magic matches, or the table is corrupt with the code `sst.magic`. A file whose magic is wrong is not a tidewater table, and nothing else in it is interpreted.
                - The format version is one the reader reads, 3 to 6 for the current release, or the table is refused with the code `sst.version`, as @refusing-to-open describes. This refusal does not wrap `ErrCorrupt`.
                - The three handles are valid and do not overlap, or the table is corrupt with the code `sst.handle`.
            }

            # Example: A Footer @footer-example
            {
                The footer of the example table, at offset 396 of a 456-byte file:

                ```
                0000018c  69 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 1e 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  i...............
                0000019c  5d 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 07 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  ]...............
                000001ac  69 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 fb 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  i...............
                000001bc  06 00 00 00 54 49 44 45 2d 53 53 54              ....TIDE-SST
                ```

                {[table]
                    Field | Value
                    Index block | Offset 361, 30 bytes
                    Filter block | Offset 93, 7 bytes
                    Properties block | Offset 105, 251 bytes
                    Format version | 6
                    Magic | `TIDE-SST`
                }
            }
        }

        # Iterating a Table @table-iteration
        {
            A range read walks a table in key order and does not consult its filter:

            {[#]
                - Binary-search the index for the first separator greater than or equal to the lookup key of the lower bound, as for a point read.
                - Read that data block and seek in it to the lower bound, as @block-search describes.
                - Decode entries forward. At the restart array, take the next index entry and read its data block from its first entry.
                - Stop at the first key at or past the upper bound, or after the last index entry.
            }

            A table yields internal keys, every version of every key in the range. Merging them with the other tables and the memtables, and showing only the newest visible version of each key, is the engine's work, described in [Iterators](../architecture.sdoc#iterators). Every block a scan reads is verified like any other read, and the prefetch the engine does on long scans reads the next block's handle from the pinned index ahead of time; neither changes anything on disk.
        }

        # Opening a Table @opening-a-table
        {
            A reader opens a table when the manifest lists it, either because the database is opening or because an edit has just added it. It reads only the footer and the three blocks the footer names; data blocks are read on demand.

            {[#]
                - Read the last 60 bytes and check the footer, as @sstable-footer gives the order.
                - Read the index block, check its trailer and decode it, checking that its separators ascend.
                - Read the filter block, check its trailer and its probe count.
                - Read the properties block, check its trailer, and check that every property the table's version requires is present and consistent, and that its sequence numbers agree with the manifest's `add-table`.
                - Pin the index and filter blocks for as long as the table is live. They do not count against the block cache, as [Block Cache](../architecture.sdoc#block-cache) says.
            }

            A table the manifest lists that fails any of these checks makes the database refuse to open, with an error naming the table and wrapping `ErrCorrupt`:

            ```
            tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: sst/000951.sst: bad magic 0x0000000000000000, want 0x5453532d45444954
            ```

            A table that opened cleanly and later fails the checksum of a data block does not stop the database: only reads of that block's key range fail, and the table is marked suspect with a `suspect-table` edit, as [Silent Corruption](../architecture.sdoc#corruption) describes.
        }

        # Validation Rules @sstable-validation
        {
            {[table]
                Code | Check that failed | Found | Message
                `sst.size` | The file is shorter than its 60-byte footer | At open | `sst/000951.sst: file is 12 bytes, shorter than the footer`
                `sst.magic` | The last 8 bytes are not `TIDE-SST` | At open | `sst/000951.sst: bad magic 0x0000000000000000, want 0x5453532d45444954`
                `sst.version` | The footer's format version is not one the reader reads | At open | `sst/000951.sst: format version 7 is newer than this release reads (3 to 6)`
                `sst.handle` | A handle has size 0, runs into the footer, or overlaps another | At open, or at first use | `sst/000951.sst: index handle (offset 361, size 9000) runs past the footer at 396`
                `sst.checksum` | A block's stored CRC32C differs from the computed one | On every block read | `sst/000951.sst: block at offset 0: checksum mismatch (stored 0x8fc5d04f, computed 0x1b2e44c0)`
                `sst.compression` | A compression byte other than 0 or 1, or 1 on a block that is not a data block, or in a table of format 3 | On every block read | `sst/000951.sst: block at offset 93: compression 1 on a filter block`
                `sst.snappy` | A Snappy block does not decode, or decodes to another length than it declares | On a data block read | `sst/000951.sst: block at offset 0: snappy: corrupt input`
                `sst.block` | A restart array or an entry does not fit its block, or a `shared` exceeds the previous key | On every block decode | `sst/000951.sst: block at offset 0: entry at 37 runs into the restart array`
                `sst.order` | A key is not greater than the one before it, in a block, across blocks, or in the index | On decode | `sst/000951.sst: block at offset 0: key at 25 does not sort after the key before it`
                `sst.key` | An internal key shorter than 8 bytes, an unknown kind, or a value that does not fit its kind | On decode | `sst/000951.sst: block at offset 0: entry at 37 has kind 2 in a table of format 5`
                `sst.filter` | The filter block is shorter than 2 bytes, or its probe count is 0 or over 30 | At open | `sst/000951.sst: filter block has 0 probes`
                `sst.property` | A required property is missing, does not decode as one varint, or is inconsistent | At open | `sst/000951.sst: missing property tidewater.num-entries`
            }

            Every message is prefixed with `tidewater: ` and, at open, with the database directory, as in @opening-a-table. Every error except `sst.version` wraps `ErrCorrupt`, so `errors.Is(err, tidewater.ErrCorrupt)` holds for it. A checksum failure also increments `tidewater_block_checksum_failures_total`, whether the read came from a get, an iterator, a compaction or the scrubber.
        }

        # Example: A Whole Table @sstable-example
        {
            The complete `sst/000951.sst` used in the examples above, 456 bytes, written by a flush in format version 6:

            ```
            00000000  00 11 05 75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 35 01 61 9a 13  ...user:1005.a..
            00000010  00 00 00 00 61 6c 69 63 65 08 09 00 36 00 62 9a  ....alice...6.b.
            00000020  13 00 00 00 00 07 0a 0b 31 37 02 6d 9a 13 00 00  ........17.m....
            00000030  00 00 00 38 d3 60 ba 01 00 00 62 6f 62 06 0b 05  ...8.`....bob...
            00000040  31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00 63 61 72 6f 6c  100.}......carol
            00000050  00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 4f d0 c5 8f aa ae 1b  .........O......
            00000060  d6 09 49 08 00 0e 29 f4 a8 00 1c 01 74 69 64 65  ..I...).....tide
            00000070  77 61 74 65 72 2e 62 6c 6f 6f 6d 2d 62 69 74 73  water.bloom-bits
            00000080  2d 70 65 72 2d 6b 65 79 0c 0a 0f 01 63 72 65 61  -per-key....crea
            00000090  74 69 6f 6e 2d 72 65 61 73 6f 6e 01 0a 09 01 64  tion-reason....d
            000000a0  61 74 61 2d 73 69 7a 65 5d 0a 0f 06 65 61 72 6c  ata-size]...earl
            000000b0  69 65 73 74 2d 65 78 70 69 72 79 80 f0 cc 86 a6  iest-expiry.....
            000000c0  37 0a 0d 03 6c 61 72 67 65 73 74 2d 73 65 71 6e  7...largest-seqn
            000000d0  6f fd b4 4e 0a 0f 01 6e 75 6d 2d 64 61 74 61 2d  o..N...num-data-
            000000e0  62 6c 6f 63 6b 73 01 0f 08 01 65 6c 65 74 69 6f  blocks....eletio
            000000f0  6e 73 01 0e 07 01 65 6e 74 72 69 65 73 04 0f 07  ns....entries...
            00000100  01 78 70 69 72 69 6e 67 01 0a 0c 01 72 61 77 2d  .xpiring....raw-
            00000110  6b 65 79 2d 73 69 7a 65 44 0e 0a 01 76 61 6c 75  key-sizeD...valu
            00000120  65 2d 73 69 7a 65 15 0a 0a 01 73 65 65 6b 2d 63  e-size....seek-c
            00000130  6f 75 6e 74 00 0b 0d 03 6d 61 6c 6c 65 73 74 2d  ount....mallest-
            00000140  73 65 71 6e 6f e1 b4 4e 0a 0f 02 74 6f 6d 62 73  seqno..N...tombs
            00000150  74 6f 6e 65 2d 73 68 61 72 65 c4 13 00 00 00 00  tone-share......
            00000160  01 00 00 00 00 4a fd 37 68 00 11 02 75 73 65 72  .....J.7h...user
            00000170  3a 31 31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00 00 58 00  :1100.}.......X.
            00000180  00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 05 a2 5c 7b 69 01 00 00  ..........\{i...
            00000190  00 00 00 00 1e 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 5d 00 00 00  ............]...
            000001a0  00 00 00 00 07 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 69 00 00 00  ............i...
            000001b0  00 00 00 00 fb 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 06 00 00 00  ................
            000001c0  54 49 44 45 2d 53 53 54                          TIDE-SST
            ```

            {[table]
                Offset | Size | Part
                0 | 88 + 5 | Data block and trailer, @data-block-example
                93 | 7 + 5 | Filter block and trailer, @filter-example
                105 | 251 + 5 | Properties block and trailer, @properties-example
                361 | 30 + 5 | Index block and trailer, @index-example
                396 | 60 | Footer, @footer-example
            }

            The whole file has the CRC32C `0xed93652e`, which is not stored anywhere but lets an implementation check that it reproduced the file byte for byte.
        }
    }

    # Manifest Format @manifest-format
    {
        The manifest is an append-only file of version edits, and `CURRENT` is a one-line file naming the live manifest. Replaying the edits of the live manifest from its start gives the current version: the live tables at each level, the next file number, the last sequence number, the oldest log segment still needed, and the last checkpoint.

        How the engine writes edits, serialises them and rotates the file is in [the manifest component](../components/manifest.sdoc), and the size at which the manifest is rotated is in [Manifest](../architecture.sdoc#manifest). This section gives the bytes, the replay rules and the checks.

        # The CURRENT File @current-file
        {
            `CURRENT` holds the name of the live manifest followed by a single newline, and nothing else:

            ```
            00000000  4d 41 4e 49 46 45 53 54 2d 30 30 30 30 34 32 0a  MANIFEST-000042.
            ```

            A writer replaces it whole: it writes `CURRENT.tmp`, fsyncs it, renames it to `CURRENT` and fsyncs the database directory. A reader therefore finds either the old name or the new one, never a mixture.

            A reader checks, in order:

            {[#]
                - `CURRENT` exists. If it does not, and the directory holds no manifest either, the directory is not a database yet, and the engine creates one there. If it does not but a manifest exists, the database is damaged, and the reader refuses with the code `current.missing`: it must never create a new database over an old one.
                - Its content is `MANIFEST-`, a file number of six digits or more, and one newline, with no other bytes, no carriage return and no second line. Otherwise the reader refuses with the code `current.syntax`.
                - The manifest it names exists. Otherwise the reader refuses with the code `manifest.missing`.
            }

            Any other `MANIFEST-NNNNNN` in the directory is an orphan of an interrupted rotation, and is deleted on open, as @orphans says. A reader that only inspects leaves it in place.
        }

        # Manifest Files @manifest-files
        {
            A manifest is the file `MANIFEST-NNNNNN` in the database directory, named after a number from the shared counter of @file-numbers. A new database's first manifest is `MANIFEST-000002`, since its first log segment took number 1.

            A manifest is a sequence of edits, back to back, from offset 0 to the end of the file. It has no header of its own, no blocks and no padding: unlike the log, it is not divided into 32 KiB blocks, and an edit is never split. The first edit is what identifies the file, since it must start with the `format-version` field.

            The engine appends one edit per flush, compaction, checkpoint and suspect table, fsyncs the file after each, and applies an edit in memory only once its fsync has returned. A crash can therefore cut short the edit being written, but never leave the engine running on an edit that is not on disk.
        }

        # Edit Framing @edit-framing
        {
            Every edit is a 12-byte header followed by its body:

            ```
            +---------+---------+-------------------+
            | length  | xxh3-64 | body              |
            | 4 bytes | 8 bytes | length bytes      |
            +---------+---------+-------------------+
            ```

            {[table]
                Offset | Size | Field | Encoding | Meaning
                0 | 4 | length | `uint32` | Length of the body in bytes, at least 1
                4 | 8 | checksum | `uint64` | xxh3-64 of the body, with seed 0
                12 | length | body | fields | The edit's fields, as @manifest-fields gives them
            }

            The checksum covers the body only. As with the log, a damaged length makes the checksum run over the wrong bytes, so it fails all the same.

            The manifest does not use CRC32C, unlike the log records and table blocks that [D-0004](../decisions.sdoc#d-0004) covers: a manifest edit that replays wrongly loses whole tables, and the 64-bit checksum makes an undetected error in one far less likely. The length is a `uint32`, so one edit holds at most 4 GiB; the largest edit, the first of a rotated manifest, which lists every live table, stays far below that.
        }

        # Edit Fields @manifest-fields
        {
            The body of an edit is a sequence of fields. Each field is a tag, a varint, followed by a value whose encoding the tag fixes. Fields have no length of their own, so a reader cannot skip a field it does not know, and a tag it does not know makes the manifest corrupt, with the code `manifest.tag`.

            {[table]
                Tag | Field | Value | At most once per edit | Written in
                1 | `format-version` | Varint version, then the oldest release that reads it as length-prefixed bytes | Yes | The first edit of every manifest, as its first field, and nowhere else
                2 | `log-number` | Varint: the number of the oldest log segment that may hold records no table has | Yes | Flush edits, recovery checkpoints, and the first edit of a manifest
                3 | `next-file-number` | Varint: the lowest file number not yet allocated | Yes | Every edit that allocates a file, and the first edit of a manifest
                4 | `last-sequence` | Varint: the highest sequence number in any table written so far | Yes | Flush and compaction edits, and the first edit of a manifest
                5 | `delete-table` | Varint level, then varint file number | No | Compaction edits
                6 | `add-table` | As in @add-table-field | No | Flush and compaction edits, and the first edit of a rotated manifest
                7 | `checkpoint` | Varint: the highest sequence number whose effects are durable in tables | Yes | Checkpoint edits, and the first edit of a rotated manifest
                8 | `suspect-table` | Varint file number of a live table with a corrupt block | No | Edits after a checksum failure, and the first edit of a rotated manifest
            }

            Tag 0 and every tag above 8 are corrupt. A field that may appear at most once and appears twice in one edit is corrupt, with the code `manifest.field`, and so is a varint or length-prefixed value that runs past the end of the body, or a body with bytes left after its last complete field.

            Fields are applied in the order written. A writer writes the `delete-table` fields of an edit before its `add-table` fields, so that a table moved from one level to another is deleted from the first before it is added to the second.

            The release in `format-version` is text such as `2.3`: the oldest release that reads the manifest's format version. A release that meets a version it does not know cannot know which release introduced it, so the manifest tells it, and the release names it in its refusal, as @refusing-to-open shows.

            # The add-table Field @add-table-field
            {
                {[table]
                    Field | Encoding | Meaning
                    level | varint | The level the table goes to, 0 for a flush
                    file number | varint | The table's number: its file is `sst/NNNNNN.sst`
                    size | varint | The size of the table's file in bytes
                    smallest | length-prefixed bytes | The smallest internal key in the table
                    largest | length-prefixed bytes | The largest internal key in the table
                    smallest seqno | varint | The lowest sequence number in the table
                    largest seqno | varint | The highest sequence number in the table
                }

                The keys are internal keys, trailer included, so they are at least 8 bytes long. The sequence numbers must agree with the table's own `tidewater.smallest-seqno` and `tidewater.largest-seqno` properties, which the engine checks when it opens the table, and the size with the file's size on disk, which `tidewater-admin manifest verify` checks.

                The level is not stored anywhere in the table. A table moved from one level to another without being rewritten is a `delete-table` at the old level and an `add-table` with the same number at the new one, in one edit.
            }
        }

        # Replaying a Manifest @manifest-replay-rules
        {
            A reader replays a manifest by applying its edits, in order, to an empty version. It verifies each edit's checksum before it applies anything in the edit. The state it keeps:

            {[table]
                State | Starts as | Changed by
                Format version and release | Unset | `format-version`, in the first edit only
                Live tables, by level | None | `add-table` and `delete-table`
                Suspect tables | None | `suspect-table`, and `delete-table` of the table
                Next file number | Unset | `next-file-number`
                Last sequence number | 0 | `last-sequence`
                Log number | Unset | `log-number`
                Checkpoint | 0, meaning none | `checkpoint`
            }

            Each edit must leave the state consistent. An edit that breaks one of these rules makes the manifest corrupt, with the code `manifest.state`, and the reader refuses to open:

            {[.]
                - The first edit sets `format-version`, `log-number` and `next-file-number`; no other edit sets `format-version`.
                - `add-table` names a file number that is not already live, and below the next file number in effect after the edit.
                - `delete-table` names a table that is live at the level given.
                - `suspect-table` names a live table.
                - `next-file-number`, `last-sequence`, `log-number` and `checkpoint` never decrease from one edit to the next.
                - Every `add-table` has a smallest key not greater than its largest key, and a smallest sequence number not greater than its largest.
            }

            A table deleted by `delete-table` stops being suspect with it. A suspect table stays live and readable outside its damaged range; the mark tells the engine to fetch the range again on a replicated database, as [Silent Corruption](../architecture.sdoc#corruption) describes, and tells `tidewater-admin info` to report it.

            Once every edit has been applied, the engine checks that every live table exists under `sst/` with the size its `add-table` recorded, and opens it as @opening-a-table describes. A missing or shorter table makes the database refuse to open with the code `manifest.table-missing`, naming the table.
        }

        # Torn Edit at the Tail @manifest-torn-tail
        {
            The manifest has its own rule for an edit that fails, distinct from the log's. An edit is torn when it is the last thing in the file and it fails: fewer than 12 bytes of header remain, the body runs past the end of the file, or the body is all there and fails its checksum with nothing after it.

            {[table]
                Where the bad edit is | What a reader does
                At the tail, cut short or failing its checksum, with nothing after it | Ignores the edit and uses the version before it. The engine truncates the file after the last good edit before it appends anything new
                Anywhere else | Refuses to open, naming the manifest and the offset of the edit, with the code `manifest.checksum`
            }

            The first edit of a manifest is never torn in a manifest that `CURRENT` names, since `CURRENT` is pointed at a manifest only after the manifest has been fsynced. A reader that finds the first edit bad refuses with the code `manifest.checksum`, wherever the file ends.

            The two cases are told apart by position alone. A torn tail is the expected result of a crash during an append, and ignoring it loses nothing, since the edit was never applied in memory and the operation it described never completed. A bad edit followed by more bytes cannot come from a crash, since each edit is appended only after the previous one's fsync: it means corruption, and applying the edits after it would build a version that never existed.

            The engine logs the two cases at different levels:

            ```
            level=WARN msg="manifest: ignoring torn edit at tail" manifest=MANIFEST-000042 offset=42502 bytes=9
            level=ERROR msg="manifest: corrupt edit before tail" manifest=MANIFEST-000042 offset=18233
            ```

            The second is followed by `Open` failing with:

            ```
            tidewater: manifest MANIFEST-000042: corrupt edit at offset 18233 is not the last; refusing to open
            ```

            {[table]
                File | A bad unit at the tail | A bad unit before the tail | Checksum
                Log segment | Truncated, with everything after it in the segment | Truncated, with everything after it; later segments are not replayed | CRC32C per record
                Manifest | Ignored, the version before it used | The database refuses to open | xxh3-64 per edit
            }

            The difference is deliberate. A log segment can lose a tail of writes that were never acknowledged as durable, and the database is still consistent without them. A manifest that skipped an edit in its middle would be inconsistent, since every later edit was computed from the version that included it.

            A tool that only inspects, such as `tidewater-admin manifest dump`, must not truncate a torn tail. `tidewater-admin manifest verify` reports it as a warning, and exits non-zero only for a bad edit before the tail.
        }

        # Rotation @manifest-rotation-format
        {
            When the manifest grows past the size given in [Manifest](../architecture.sdoc#manifest), the engine starts a new one under a new number. The new manifest's first edit describes the whole current version on its own:

            {[table]
                Field | Value in the first edit of a rotated manifest
                `format-version` | The writer's format version, and the oldest release that reads it
                `log-number` | The current log number
                `next-file-number` | The current next file number, which already counts the new manifest's own number
                `last-sequence` | The current last sequence number
                `checkpoint` | The last checkpoint, if there has been one
                `add-table` | One for every live table, level by level
                `suspect-table` | One for every suspect table
            }

            The new manifest is fsynced before `CURRENT` is pointed at it, and the old one is deleted only once `CURRENT` is durable, so a crash at any step leaves one complete manifest that `CURRENT` names. The steps, and what a crash at each one leaves, are in [Rotation](../components/manifest.sdoc#manifest-rotation).

            A rotation never carries over a torn tail, an ignored edit, or a table that has been deleted: the first edit is built from the version in memory, not copied from the old file.
        }

        # Example: Manifest Edits @manifest-example
        {
            The first edit of a new database, at offset 0 of `MANIFEST-000002`. Its body is 12 bytes: format version 6, readable from release 2.3, log number 1, next file number 3, last sequence number 0.

            ```
            00000000  0c 00 00 00 97 30 eb 53 96 8d f3 ae 01 06 03 32  .....0.S.......2
            00000010  2e 33 02 01 03 03 04 00                          .3......
            ```

            {[table]
                Bytes | Meaning
                `0c 00 00 00` | Body length 12
                `97 30 eb 53 96 8d f3 ae` | xxh3-64 `0xaef38d9653eb3097`
                `01 06 03 32 2e 33` | `format-version` 6, release `2.3`
                `02 01` | `log-number` 1
                `03 03` | `next-file-number` 3
                `04 00` | `last-sequence` 0
            }

            The edit that records the flush of `sst/000951.sst`, the table of @sstable-example, appended at offset 42,432 of `MANIFEST-000042`. The flushed memtable's records were in `wal/000949.log`, and the active segment is `wal/000950.log`:

            ```
            0000a5c0  3a 00 00 00 a2 6b aa 99 3f 6b e1 21 06 00 b7 07  :....k..?k.!....
            0000a5d0  c8 03 11 75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 35 01 61 9a 13  ...user:1005.a..
            0000a5e0  00 00 00 00 11 75 73 65 72 3a 31 31 30 30 01 7d  .....user:1100.}
            0000a5f0  9a 13 00 00 00 00 e1 b4 4e fd b4 4e 02 b6 07 03  ........N..N....
            0000a600  b8 07 04 fd b4 4e                                .....N
            ```

            {[table]
                Bytes | Meaning
                `3a 00 00 00` | Body length 58
                `a2 6b aa 99 3f 6b e1 21` | xxh3-64 `0x21e16b3f99aa6ba2`
                `06 00 b7 07 c8 03` | `add-table`: level 0, file 951, 456 bytes
                `11 75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 35 01 61 9a 13 00 00 00 00` | Smallest key: `user:1005` at 1,284,705, put
                `11 75 73 65 72 3a 31 31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00` | Largest key: `user:1100` at 1,284,733, put
                `e1 b4 4e fd b4 4e` | Sequence numbers 1,284,705 to 1,284,733
                `02 b6 07` | `log-number` 950: `wal/000949.log` may now be deleted
                `03 b8 07` | `next-file-number` 952
                `04 fd b4 4e` | `last-sequence` 1,284,733
            }

            Had this flush been forced by a checkpoint, the next edit, at offset 42,502, would record it:

            ```
            0000a606  04 00 00 00 35 49 4f 28 af 98 1d 58 07 fd b4 4e  ....5IO(...X...N
            ```

            That is a body of 4 bytes with the xxh3-64 `0x581d98af284f4935`, holding `checkpoint` 1,284,733 as `07 fd b4 4e`. Had the crash come after only its first 9 bytes, `04 00 00 00 35 49 4f 28 af`, the edit would be torn: the reader would ignore it, use the version as of the flush edit, and log `bytes=9` at offset 42,502.

            Two more bodies, without their headers:

            {[table]
                Body | Meaning
                `08 b7 07` | `suspect-table` 951: a block of `sst/000951.sst` failed its checksum
                `05 00 b7 07 05 01 90 07` | `delete-table` level 0 file 951, and `delete-table` level 1 file 912, as the start of a compaction edit
            }
        }

        # Example: A Compaction Edit @compaction-edit-example
        {
            A compaction merges `sst/000951.sst`, on level 0, with `sst/000912.sst`, on level 1, into one new table, `sst/000953.sst`, on level 1. It is recorded in one edit, appended at offset 42,518, right after the checkpoint edit of @manifest-example:

            ```
            0000a616  40 00 00 00 7e eb 8b c7 26 5d 37 b4 05 00 b7 07  @...~...&]7.....
            0000a626  05 01 90 07 06 01 b9 07 ff da 6f 11 75 73 65 72  ..........o.user
            0000a636  3a 30 39 35 30 01 3e 02 12 00 00 00 00 11 75 73  :0950.>.......us
            0000a646  65 72 3a 31 32 31 30 00 a0 87 13 00 00 00 00 be  er:1210.........
            0000a656  84 48 fd b4 4e 03 ba 07 04 fd b4 4e              .H..N......N
            ```

            {[table]
                Bytes | Meaning
                `40 00 00 00` | Body length 64
                `7e eb 8b c7 26 5d 37 b4` | xxh3-64 `0xb4375d26c78beb7e`
                `05 00 b7 07` | `delete-table`: level 0, file 951
                `05 01 90 07` | `delete-table`: level 1, file 912
                `06 01 b9 07 ff da 6f` | `add-table`: level 1, file 953, 1,830,271 bytes
                `11 75 73 65 72 3a 30 39 35 30 01 3e 02 12 00 00 00 00` | Smallest key: `user:0950` at 1,180,222, put
                `11 75 73 65 72 3a 31 32 31 30 00 a0 87 13 00 00 00 00` | Largest key: `user:1210` at 1,279,904, delete
                `be 84 48 fd b4 4e` | Sequence numbers 1,180,222 to 1,284,733
                `03 ba 07` | `next-file-number` 954
                `04 fd b4 4e` | `last-sequence` 1,284,733, unchanged
            }

            The two deletions come before the addition, as @manifest-fields requires. A reader that applied this edit without the flush edit before it would find no live table 951 at level 0, and would refuse the manifest with `manifest.state`.
        }

        # Example: The First Edit of a Rotated Manifest @rotated-manifest-example
        {
            After the recovery of @replay-example, the engine rotates the manifest into `MANIFEST-000958`. Its first edit describes the whole version: five live tables, one of them suspect, the recovery checkpoint, the log number 957 and the next file number 959, which already counts the manifest's own number.

            ```
            00000000  02 01 00 00 e9 de 76 5e ad df 14 6c 01 06 03 32  ......v^...l...2
            00000010  2e 33 02 bd 07 03 bf 07 04 89 bf 4f 07 89 bf 4f  .3.........O...O
            00000020  06 00 bb 07 fe e5 ee 01 11 75 73 65 72 3a 30 30  .........user:00
            00000030  30 31 01 21 af 13 00 00 00 00 11 75 73 65 72 3a  01.!.......user:
            00000040  30 38 39 39 01 2a dd 13 00 00 00 00 fe b4 4e f8  0899.*........N.
            00000050  ba 4f 06 00 bc 07 8b ac 07 11 75 73 65 72 3a 30  .O........user:0
            00000060  30 31 32 00 e6 dd 13 00 00 00 00 11 75 73 65 72  012.........user
            00000070  3a 30 38 37 34 01 62 de 13 00 00 00 00 f9 ba 4f  :0874.b........O
            00000080  89 bf 4f 06 01 b9 07 ff da 6f 11 75 73 65 72 3a  ..O......o.user:
            00000090  30 39 35 30 01 3e 02 12 00 00 00 00 11 75 73 65  0950.>.......use
            000000a0  72 3a 31 32 31 30 00 a0 87 13 00 00 00 00 be 84  r:1210..........
            000000b0  48 fd b4 4e 06 02 ed 06 87 b8 e6 1b 09 61 01 0c  H..N.........a..
            000000c0  00 00 00 00 00 00 11 75 73 65 72 3a 30 39 34 39  .......user:0949
            000000d0  01 79 fd 11 00 00 00 00 0c f9 fa 47 06 02 a3 07  .y.........G....
            000000e0  d1 fd fb 13 11 75 73 65 72 3a 30 39 35 30 01 6a  .....user:0950.j
            000000f0  01 12 00 00 00 00 0e 7a 6f 6e 65 3a 39 01 c0 7c  .......zone:9..|
            00000100  13 00 00 00 00 ea 82 48 c0 f9 4d 08 a3 07        .......H..M...
            ```

            {[table]
                Bytes | Meaning
                `02 01 00 00` | Body length 258
                `e9 de 76 5e ad df 14 6c` | xxh3-64 `0x6c14dfad5e76dee9`
                `01 06 03 32 2e 33` | `format-version` 6, release `2.3`
                `02 bd 07` | `log-number` 957
                `03 bf 07` | `next-file-number` 959
                `04 89 bf 4f` | `last-sequence` 1,302,409
                `07 89 bf 4f` | `checkpoint` 1,302,409
                `06 00 bb 07 fe e5 ee 01` and keys | `add-table`: level 0, file 955, 3,912,446 bytes, `user:0001` to `user:0899`, sequence numbers 1,284,734 to 1,301,880
                `06 00 bc 07 8b ac 07` and keys | `add-table`: level 0, file 956, 120,331 bytes, `user:0012` to `user:0874`, sequence numbers 1,301,881 to 1,302,409
                `06 01 b9 07 ff da 6f` and keys | `add-table`: level 1, file 953, as in @compaction-edit-example
                `06 02 ed 06 87 b8 e6 1b` and keys | `add-table`: level 2, file 877, 58,301,447 bytes, `a` to `user:0949`
                `06 02 a3 07 d1 fd fb 13` and keys | `add-table`: level 2, file 931, 41,877,201 bytes, `user:0950` to `zone:9`
                `08 a3 07` | `suspect-table` 931
            }

            The level 0 tables come first, newest last, then each level in key order. A reader must not depend on that order, which is the writer's choice; it applies the fields as they come.
        }

        # Reading Edits with tidewater-admin @manifest-dump-example
        {
            `tidewater-admin manifest dump DIR` prints the live manifest's edits one per line, with their offsets, then the version they add up to. It takes no lock and only reads. Before the rotation, the tail of its output for the edits of this section reads:

            ```
            @42432   next-file-number=952 add-table=L0:000951 seq=1284705-1284733
            @42502   checkpoint=1284733
            @42518   next-file-number=954 delete-table=L0:000951,L1:000912 add-table=L1:000953
            ```

            The dump shows the fields an operator reads and leaves out `log-number` and `last-sequence`, which only replay needs; it shortens `add-table` to its level, file and sequence numbers. `tidewater-admin manifest verify DIR` checks everything the dump leaves out: every checksum, and every table's existence and size.
        }

        # Validation Rules @manifest-validation
        {
            {[table]
                Code | Check that failed | Message
                `current.missing` | No `CURRENT`, though the directory holds a manifest | `tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: CURRENT is missing but MANIFEST-000042 exists; refusing to create a database over it`
                `current.syntax` | `CURRENT` is not one manifest name and a newline | `tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: CURRENT: want "MANIFEST-NNNNNN\n", found 23 bytes`
                `manifest.missing` | The manifest `CURRENT` names does not exist | `tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: CURRENT names MANIFEST-000042, which does not exist`
                `manifest.checksum` | An edit fails, and is not the last thing in the file | `tidewater: manifest MANIFEST-000042: corrupt edit at offset 18233 is not the last; refusing to open`
                `manifest.tag` | A field has tag 0, or a tag above 8 | `tidewater: manifest MANIFEST-000042: edit at offset 42432: unknown tag 23`
                `manifest.field` | A field repeats where it may not, or does not fit the body | `tidewater: manifest MANIFEST-000042: edit at offset 42432: next-file-number appears twice`
                `manifest.state` | An edit breaks a rule of @manifest-replay-rules | `tidewater: manifest MANIFEST-000042: edit at offset 42432: add-table 951 is already live`
                `manifest.version` | The format version is not one the release reads | `tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: MANIFEST-000042 is format version 6, newer than this release reads (3 to 5); the oldest release that reads it is 2.3`
                `manifest.table-missing` | A live table is absent, or shorter than recorded | `tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: sst/000951.sst is listed in MANIFEST-000042 but missing`
            }

            Every one of these refuses to open the database and changes nothing on disk. All but `manifest.version` wrap `ErrCorrupt`. A torn tail is not in the table: it is not an error, only the warning of @manifest-torn-tail.
        }
    }

    # Checkpoint Records @checkpoint-records
    {
        A checkpoint records the highest sequence number whose effects are durable in tables. It is what bounds recovery: on open, the engine replays only the log records after the last checkpoint, as [Checkpoints](../architecture.sdoc#wal-checkpoints) describes, together with how often checkpoints are written.

        In the format, a checkpoint is a manifest field, `checkpoint`, written in an edit of its own. There is no checkpoint file and nothing in the log marks one: a reader learns where replay starts from the manifest alone, and then reads the log as [Write-Ahead Log Format](#write-ahead-log-format) specifies.

        # Encoding @checkpoint-encoding
        {
            {[table]
                Field | Tag | Value
                `checkpoint` | 7 | Varint: every entry with a sequence number at or below it is in a table the manifest lists, or was dropped by a compaction that had the right to drop it
                `log-number` | 2 | Optional in a checkpoint edit, present in a recovery checkpoint: the first segment replay may read
            }

            A checkpoint edit holds `checkpoint` alone, or `checkpoint` and `log-number`. The edit `07 fd b4 4e` records a checkpoint at 1,284,733, as in @manifest-example.

            Checkpoints only move forward: a `checkpoint` lower than the one before it makes the manifest corrupt, with the code `manifest.state`. The first edit of a rotated manifest carries the last checkpoint, so rotation never loses it.
        }

        # When Checkpoints Are Written @checkpoint-occasions
        {
            {[table]
                Occasion | What precedes the checkpoint edit | Value recorded
                The log has grown by the checkpoint interval since the last one | A flush of the active memtable, forced, and its flush edit | The highest sequence number in that memtable
                A clean `Close` | A flush of the active memtable, if it holds anything | The last sequence number assigned
                The end of recovery | The flush of the replayed memtable | The last sequence number replayed
            }

            The interval itself, `CheckpointInterval`, and why it bounds recovery, are in [Checkpoints](../architecture.sdoc#wal-checkpoints) and [Configuration](../architecture.sdoc#configuration). The checkpoint edit always comes after the edit that makes its flush durable, never in the same edit, so a crash between the two leaves a database whose last checkpoint is the previous one, which is still correct, only less recent.

            A database that has never been checkpointed has no `checkpoint` field anywhere in its manifest, and its checkpoint is 0: replay applies every record in the segments it reads.
        }

        # The Recovery Checkpoint @recovery-checkpoint
        {
            The checkpoint written at the end of recovery also carries `log-number`, set to the number of the segment the engine opened for new writes after recovery. That number is higher than every segment already in `wal/`, since numbers come from the shared counter, so the recovery checkpoint puts every older segment out of reach of replay:

            {[.]
                - the segments recovery replayed, whose records the recovery flush has made durable;
                - the segment it truncated, if any;
                - the segments after a truncated one, which were not replayed because the order of visibility would no longer hold.
            }

            Without it, a later open would read the segments after a truncation and apply records that follow a gap. With it, they are obsolete and the next open deletes them, as @orphans says, which is why [the WAL recovery runbook](../runbooks/wal-recovery.sdoc#remedies) asks for a copy of the directory before a second open.

            In @replay-example, the last batch replayed is 1,302,409, and the engine opens `wal/000957.log` for new writes. The recovery checkpoint then has the body `07 89 bf 4f 02 bd 07`, `checkpoint` 1,302,409 and `log-number` 957, and is written as:

            ```
            07 00 00 00 94 9a 1b 6b 01 0b d3 e8 07 89 bf 4f 02 bd 07
            ```
        }

        # Where Replay Starts @replay-start
        {
            On open, once the manifest has been replayed, the engine knows the last checkpoint `C`, 0 if there is none, and the log number `L`. It replays the log as follows:

            {[#]
                - List the segments in `wal/` and keep those numbered `L` or higher, in ascending order of their numbers. Gaps between the numbers are normal, since tables and manifests take numbers from the same counter.
                - Read each segment as @log-reading describes, stopping at the first bad record of any segment, and replaying no segment after it.
                - Skip every batch whose last sequence number is at or below `C`: its effects are already in tables.
                - Apply every later batch to a fresh memtable. The first batch applied may start anywhere above `C`, since flushes after the checkpoint may have deleted the segments that held the numbers in between; from then on, the batches must be contiguous, as @log-sequence-numbers requires.
            }

            A batch that straddles `C`, starting at or below it and ending above it, cannot occur, since a flush always takes whole batches, and it is reported as a bad record of the kind `log.sequence`.

            The engine logs where replay starts, with the checkpoint and the amount of log to read, before it reads anything:

            ```
            level=INFO msg="wal replay start" checkpoint_seqno=88731220 segments=4 bytes=241172480
            ```

            After replay, the next sequence number the engine assigns is one more than the highest of the manifest's `last-sequence`, the checkpoint, and the last sequence number replayed.

            `tidewater-admin wal ls DIR` lists the same segments that replay would read, with the first and last sequence number of each, and `tidewater-admin wal verify DIR` checks them without replaying them.
        }

        # Example: Replay After a Crash @replay-example
        {
            Continuing the examples of @manifest-example: after the compaction edit, the memtable of `wal/000950.log` was frozen, which opened `wal/000954.log`, and its flush wrote `sst/000955.sst` and moved `log-number` to 954. Then the host lost power, before `wal/000950.log` had been deleted and while a batch was being appended to `wal/000954.log`. On the next open:

            {[table]
                Manifest state after replay | Value
                `checkpoint` | 1,284,733, from the edit at offset 42,502
                `log-number` | 954, from the flush of `sst/000955.sst`
                `last-sequence` | 1,301,880
                `next-file-number` | 956
            }

            {[table]
                Segment | Holds | What replay does
                `wal/000950.log` | Batches 1,284,734 to 1,301,880, all in `sst/000955.sst` | Nothing: it is below `log-number`, and open deletes it unread
                `wal/000954.log` | Batches 1,301,881 to 1,302,410, the last one torn | Replays 1,301,881 to 1,302,409, then stops at the torn batch
            }

            The first batch replayed, 1,301,881, is far above the checkpoint, 1,284,733: the batches in between are in `sst/000955.sst`, whose segment is gone from replay. That is allowed, as @replay-start says; from 1,301,881 on, the batches must follow each other without a gap. The torn batch is reported as `log.short`, the segment is truncated at its good end, and the engine logs:

            ```
            level=INFO msg="wal replay start" checkpoint_seqno=1284733 segments=1 bytes=548213
            level=WARN msg="wal segment truncated" segment=wal/000954.log offset=548170 discarded_bytes=43
            ```

            The recovery flush then writes `sst/000956.sst` with the batches 1,301,881 to 1,302,409, the engine opens `wal/000957.log` for new writes, and the recovery checkpoint of @recovery-checkpoint records the checkpoint 1,302,409 with `log-number` 957. The next sequence number assigned is 1,302,410, the number the torn batch had. That batch was lost with the power, as a write made without `Sync: true` may be, as [Power Loss and Torn Writes](../architecture.sdoc#torn-writes) explains.
        }

        # Checkpoint Records and Checkpoint Directories @checkpoint-vs-backup
        {
            The word checkpoint also names `db.Checkpoint(dir)`, which makes a consistent copy of an open database in another directory, as [Backup and Restore](../architecture.sdoc#backup-and-restore) describes. The two are different things. A checkpoint directory is a database directory like any other, with its own `CURRENT`, manifest, tables and log segments, all in the format of this document; the checkpoint records in its manifest are the ones that tell its first open where replay starts.
        }
    }

    # Format Versions and Compatibility @format-versions
    {
        One integer, the format version, says how a database's tables and manifest are laid out. The engine writes only the newest version and reads every version from 3 up, so that a database written by an older release opens with a newer one and moves to the new format gradually. The summary is in [On-Disk Format](../architecture.sdoc#on-disk-format); this section gives what each version means to a reader, and the rules for upgrading, downgrading and refusing.

        # The Version Constant @format-constant
        {
            The format version a release writes is `TIDEWATER_FORMAT_VERSION`, in `src/format.go`, beside the oldest version the release reads and the magic number of the table footer:

            ```go
            const (
                // TIDEWATER_FORMAT_VERSION is the on-disk format this release writes,
                // in every table footer and in the first edit of every manifest.
                TIDEWATER_FORMAT_VERSION = 6

                // oldestFormatVersion is the oldest format this release reads.
                oldestFormatVersion = 3

                // tableMagic ends every table: the bytes "TIDE-SST", little-endian.
                tableMagic = 0x5453532d45444954
            )
            ```

            The current release writes 6 and reads 3 to 6. The constant changes only by the release procedure, as @changing-the-format describes.
        }

        # Where the Version Is Written @version-locations
        {
            {[table]
                File | Where | Encoding
                Table | Offset 48 of the footer | `uint32`
                Manifest | `format-version`, the first field of the first edit | Varint, followed by the oldest release that reads it
                Log segment | Nowhere | The log carries no version, as [Write-Ahead Log Format](#write-ahead-log-format) explains
                `CURRENT`, `LOCK` | Nowhere | Their layout has never changed
            }

            A table's version is the one it was written in, and never changes, since a table is never modified. A manifest's version is the one it was written in, and is fixed for the life of the file.

            A manifest never lists a table of a newer format version than its own. A writer about to record the first table of a newer version than the live manifest's starts a new manifest first, as @manifest-rotation-format describes, and records the table in that one. A reader can therefore decide from the manifest's first edit alone whether it can open the database, before it reads any table.
        }

        # Version History @version-history
        {
            {[table]
                Version | Introduced in | Change | For a reader
                3 | 1.0 | First stable format | The base of everything below
                4 | 1.6 | Snappy compression of data blocks | A compression byte in every block trailer, which may be 1 on a data block
                5 | 2.1 | Seek counts and tombstone share in the table properties | `tidewater.seek-count` and `tidewater.tombstone-share` are required
                6 | 2.3 | Expiry times on values | Entries of kind 2, in tables and in log batches; `tidewater.num-expiring` and `tidewater.earliest-expiry` are required
            }

            Versions 1 and 2 were used by development snapshots before release 1.0. No release reads them, and a file that claims one is refused like any version a release does not read.

            {[table]
                Releases | Write | Read
                1.0 to 1.5 | 3 | 3
                1.6 to 2.0 | 4 | 3 and 4
                2.1 and 2.2 | 5 | 3 to 5
                2.3 and later | 6 | 3 to 6
            }

            # Format 3 @format-3
            {
                Written by releases 1.0 to 1.5. Its tables have the layout of @sstable-format, with three differences: a block's trailer is the 4-byte CRC32C of the block alone, with no compression byte; no block is compressed; and the properties of formats 5 and 6 are absent. Its entries are of kind 0 or 1 only.

                The other files of format 3, its manifests and the log records of the releases that wrote it, are described in the superseded [format 3 specification](format-v1.sdoc). A reader of format 3 manifests needs it beside this document.
            }

            # Format 4 @format-4
            {
                Written by releases 1.6 to 2.0. It added Snappy compression of data blocks, and with it the compression byte at the start of every block trailer, which made the trailer 5 bytes long. Only data blocks may carry the value 1; the filter, properties and index blocks are always stored as they are.

                A format 4 table is laid out exactly as @sstable-format gives it, without the properties of formats 5 and 6 and without entries of kind 2.
            }

            # Format 5 @format-5
            {
                Written by releases 2.1 and 2.2. It added two required properties, `tidewater.seek-count` and `tidewater.tombstone-share`, which feed the seek and tombstone triggers of compaction. Nothing else changed: a reader that ignores the two properties reads a format 5 table as it reads a format 4 one.
            }

            # Format 6 @format-6
            {
                Written by releases 2.3 and later, the current format. It added expiry times on values: entries of kind 2 in data blocks, whose value starts with the expiry, as @expiring-values gives it; entries of kind 2 in log batches, as @log-batch-encoding gives them; and the properties `tidewater.num-expiring` and `tidewater.earliest-expiry`.

                A reader of format 5 that met a format 6 table would misread every entry of kind 2, which is why the version exists at all: it lets such a reader refuse instead.
            }
        }

        # Reading Older Tables @reading-older-formats
        {
            A reader of tables of every version from 3 to 6 handles the differences in one place, keyed on the footer's version:

            {[table]
                Version | Trailer | Compression 1 allowed | Kind 2 allowed | Required properties
                3 | 4 bytes, CRC32C only | No | No | Those of format 3
                4 | 5 bytes | On data blocks | No | Those of format 3
                5 | 5 bytes | On data blocks | No | Those of formats 3 and 5
                6 | 5 bytes | On data blocks | Yes | Those of formats 3, 5 and 6
            }

            Where an older table lacks a property a later version added, the engine behaves as follows, and a compatible reader should do the same:

            {[table]
                Missing property | Treated as
                `tidewater.seek-count` | No count: the seek trigger never fires for the table
                `tidewater.tombstone-share` | No share: the tombstone trigger never fires for the table
                `tidewater.num-expiring`, `tidewater.earliest-expiry` | The table holds no entry with an expiry, which in a table of format 3 to 5 is true
            }

            So tables written before format 5 are compacted by size alone until compaction rewrites them, as [Table Properties](../components/sstables.sdoc#sstable-properties) says.

            # Example: One Block in Format 3 and in Format 6 @trailer-versions-example
            {
                The same 64-byte data block, holding a put of `user:1005`, a delete of `user:1006` and a put of `user:1100`, has a different trailer in each version. Entries of kind 2 are left out, since format 3 cannot hold them.

                ```
                00000000  00 11 05 75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 35 01 61 9a 13  ...user:1005.a..
                00000010  00 00 00 00 61 6c 69 63 65 08 09 00 36 00 62 9a  ....alice...6.b.
                00000020  13 00 00 00 00 06 0b 05 31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00  ........100.}...
                00000030  00 00 00 63 61 72 6f 6c 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00  ...carol........
                ```

                {[table]
                    Version | Trailer | Covers | CRC32C
                    3 | `c6 a8 0d 7d` | The 64 bytes of the block | `0x7d0da8c6`
                    4 to 6 | `00 d5 9f 2c b7` | The 64 bytes, then the compression byte `00` | `0xb72c9fd5`
                }

                A reader that took the version 3 trailer for a version 6 one would read `c6` as a compression byte, and refuse the block with `sst.compression`; one that did the reverse would compute the checksum over the wrong bytes and refuse it with `sst.checksum`. Either way nothing wrong is returned, but only the footer's version gets the trailer right.
            }
        }

        # Lazy Upgrade @lazy-upgrade
        {
            A database is upgraded lazily. Opening it with a newer release changes nothing on disk: the existing tables keep their versions, and the manifest is not rewritten. From then on, every table the release writes, by a flush or a compaction, is in the newest version, and the first one it records makes the manifest rotate to the newest version as @version-locations explains.

            The older tables are rewritten in the new format only as compaction reaches them, since every compaction writes its output in the newest version, whatever its inputs: see [Compaction](../architecture.sdoc#compaction) for what decides when a key range is compacted. A key range that is rarely written may keep tables of an older version for a long time, which is harmless, since the release reads them. A manual compaction of the whole key range with `db.Compact` rewrites every table at once, at the cost of rewriting the whole database.

            There is no upgrade command, and none is needed. `tidewater-admin info DIR` reports how far an upgrade has gone, with the number of live tables of each version:

            ```
            $ tidewater-admin info /var/lib/app/db
            manifest        MANIFEST-000042   format-version 6
            tables          215
              format 6      188
              format 5      27
            suspect tables  0
            ```
        }

        # Example: Upgrading a Database Written by 2.1 @upgrade-example
        {
            A database last written by release 2.1, whose tables and manifest are all of format 5, is opened with release 2.3:

            {[table]
                Moment | On disk
                After `Open` | Nothing has changed. The manifest is of format 5 and every table is of format 5
                After the first flush | The engine rotates the manifest before recording the new table, since it is of format 6. The new manifest is of format 6, its first edit names release 2.3, and it lists one table of format 6 and every older one
                As compaction runs | Each compaction writes format 6 tables and deletes format 5 ones. `tidewater-admin info` shows the format 5 count falling
                Once compaction has reached every key range | Every table is of format 6
            }

            From the first flush on, release 2.2 refuses to open the database, naming release 2.3 in its message, and going back takes `tidewater-admin downgrade`, as @downgrade describes. Release 2.1 refuses too, but no tool takes the database back to it: 2.1 is not the previous minor release of 2.3.
        }

        # Downgrade @downgrade
        {
            Downgrades are supported to the previous minor release only, and only while no table of the newer format exists, as [On-Disk Format](../architecture.sdoc#on-disk-format) says. Going from 2.3 back to 2.2 means going from format 6 back to format 5; going from 2.4 back to 2.3, two releases that both write format 6, needs nothing.

            When the newer release has written tables in its format, which it does from its first flush, `tidewater-admin downgrade DIR` rewrites them in the older format first. It is run with the newer release's `tidewater-admin`, the one that reads both formats:

            {[#]
                - Close the database cleanly with the newer release. A clean close leaves a checkpoint with nothing after it in the log, which matters because the older release may not read the log the newer one wrote: release 2.2 cannot read entries of kind 2.
                - Run `tidewater-admin downgrade DIR`. It refuses while `LOCK` is held, and refuses a database whose `LOCK` file is still present, since that means the last close was not clean.
                - The tool rewrites every live table of the newer version in the older version, through a temporary name and a rename, with `tidewater.creation-reason` set to 3, and writes a new manifest in the older version, holding the rewritten tables, by the steps of a rotation.
                - Open the database with the older release.
            }

            Format 5 has no way to store an expiry. When it rewrites a format 6 table, the tool drops the values whose expiry has already passed, which are absent to every reader anyway, and refuses if any value with an expiry still in the future remains, naming the table, the number of such values and the first key:

            ```
            tidewater-admin downgrade: sst/001207.sst holds 3 values with an expiry that has not passed (first key "session:9"); format 5 cannot store it
            ```

            The application then deletes those keys, or waits for them to expire, and runs the tool again. The tool never turns a value with an expiry into one without, since that would make it live forever. A refusal leaves the database as it was, still in the newer format; the error code of every refusal of the tool is `format.downgrade`.

            A manifest of the newer version that lists no table of that version, after its tables were all deleted, still makes the older release refuse to open, since the release decides from the manifest. The tool then only rotates the manifest.
        }

        # Example: Downgrading from 2.3 to 2.2 @downgrade-example
        {
            A database written by release 2.3, with the five tables of @rotated-manifest-example, is taken back to release 2.2:

            ```
            $ tidewater-admin downgrade /var/lib/app/db
            checking /var/lib/app/db: closed cleanly, MANIFEST-000958 format-version 6
            sst/000955.sst  format 6 -> 5   rewritten as sst/000959.sst   dropped 12 expired values
            sst/000956.sst  format 6 -> 5   rewritten as sst/000960.sst
            sst/000953.sst  format 6 -> 5   rewritten as sst/000961.sst   dropped 1 expired value
            sst/000877.sst  format 5        kept
            sst/000931.sst  format 5        kept
            MANIFEST-000962 written, format-version 5; CURRENT updated
            ```

            The tables of format 5, written before the database was upgraded, are kept as they are. Each table of format 6 is rewritten under a new number, since numbers are never reused, and its old file is deleted once the new manifest is durable. The new manifest's first edit names release 2.1, the oldest release that reads format 5, and lists the rewritten tables in place of the old ones.
        }

        # Refusing to Open @refusing-to-open
        {
            A release refuses to open a database holding files of a format version it does not read, with an error naming the oldest release that can, and changes nothing on disk. The check happens at the manifest's first edit, so a release refuses before it reads any table:

            ```
            tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: MANIFEST-000042 is format version 6, newer than this release reads (3 to 5); the oldest release that reads it is 2.3
            ```

            The release in the message comes from the manifest itself, from the `format-version` field, since the refusing release cannot know a version newer than its own. The remedy is to open the database with that release or a later one, as [the WAL recovery runbook](../runbooks/wal-recovery.sdoc#failed-recovery) says.

            A version older than the oldest the release reads is refused the same way:

            ```
            tidewater: open /var/lib/app/db: MANIFEST-000007 is format version 2, older than this release reads (3 to 6); no release reads it
            ```

            A table whose own footer names a version the release does not read, which the manifest's version rules out unless a file was copied in by hand, is refused with the code `sst.version`. None of these refusals wraps `ErrCorrupt`: the files are not damaged, only newer or older than the release.
        }

        # Changing the Format @changing-the-format
        {
            Any change to what is written to disk increases `TIDEWATER_FORMAT_VERSION` by one. The steps are part of the release procedure, in [Releasing Tidewater](../skills/release.sdoc#steps): bump the constant, add the new version to the table in [On-Disk Format](../architecture.sdoc#on-disk-format), and add a test that opens a database written by the previous release. The format version never changes in a patch release, as [Patch Releases](../skills/release.sdoc#patch-releases) says.

            {[table]
                Change | A new format version?
                A new entry kind, record type, manifest tag or compression type | Yes
                A new required property | Yes
                Any change to a layout this document gives | Yes
                A new informational property | No: readers ignore unknown properties
                A writer's choice the format leaves open, such as the restart interval, the separators in the index or the target size of a block | No: readers accept any choice
                A new check in a reader | No, unless it refuses files that earlier releases wrote
            }

            The log has no version field of its own, so a change that reaches the log must be one that a reader of the new version can recognise in the bytes, as entries of kind 2 are, and it still takes a new format version, so that a downgrade knows to require a clean close.

            A change to the format also changes this document: the version history, @reading-older-formats, the constants of @appendix-constants, the registries of @appendix-tags and the vectors of @appendix-test-vectors, in the same pull request as the code.
        }
    }

    # Reader Conformance @reader-conformance
    {
        A reader is compatible with this format when it meets every requirement below. The engine's own code is the reference, `src/wal.go` for the log, `src/sstable.go` and `src/bloom.go` for tables and `src/manifest.go` for the manifest, and the test vectors of @appendix-test-vectors are what a new reader is checked against.

        # Requirements @conformance-must
        {
            {[#]
                - Decode every fixed-width integer as little-endian, and every varint as Go's `binary.Uvarint` does, with its three error cases.
                - Compute CRC32C with the Castagnoli polynomial and xxh3-64 with seed 0, and reproduce the check values of @checksums.
                - Check every length against what is left of its enclosing structure before using it or allocating for it.
                - Verify a log record's or a block's checksum before decoding or decompressing anything it holds, and a manifest edit's before applying any of its fields.
                - Read log segments in ascending numeric order from the manifest's `log-number`, and skip batches at or below the last checkpoint.
                - Stop reading a log at its first bad record, report the good end, and read no later segment.
                - Ignore a torn edit at the tail of a manifest, and refuse a bad edit anywhere else.
                - Take a table's trailer size, its allowed compression and its required properties from the version in its footer.
                - Refuse a format version outside the range it reads, with a message naming the version and, for a manifest, the release that reads it.
                - Accept any restart interval, any valid separator, any block size and any filter size the handles and lengths allow.
                - Ignore table properties it does not know.
                - Treat an entry whose expiry has passed as a version of its key that reads as absent, not as damage.
            }
        }

        # Prohibitions @conformance-must-not
        {
            {[.]
                - Never return bytes from a record or block whose checksum failed, not even in part.
                - Never treat a `.tmp` file, a table no live edit names, or a manifest `CURRENT` does not name as part of the database.
                - Never take `LOCK`, create `LOCK`, truncate a segment or a manifest, or delete a file, when only inspecting.
                - Never sort segment or table names as strings.
                - Never interpret the padding at the end of a log block, or report it as damage.
                - Never treat an expiry of any value as meaning "no expiry".
                - Never skip an unknown manifest tag: it has no length, and what follows it cannot be decoded.
            }
        }

        # Testing a Reader @conformance-testing
        {
            Beyond the vectors, a reader should be tested the way the engine's are, as [Testing](../architecture.sdoc#testing) describes: its record and block decoders fuzzed against arbitrary input, which must produce an error or a value and never a crash, a hang or an allocation beyond the input's size; and its log reader run over every prefix of a valid segment, each of which must decode to whole batches up to a good end, with `log.short` or no error at all.

            The negative vectors of @vectors-negative give, for each kind of damage, the input and the code a reader must report.
        }
    }

    # Appendix A: Constants @appendix-constants
    {
        Every constant of the format, in one place. A constant that tunes the engine rather than the format, such as an fsync threshold or a default size, is not here, and neither is any figure only the architecture gives.

        {[table]
            Constant | Value | Section
            Current format version, `TIDEWATER_FORMAT_VERSION` | 6 | @format-constant
            Oldest format version read | 3 | @version-history
            Byte order of fixed-width integers | Little-endian | @byte-order
            Longest varint | 10 bytes | @varints
            Digits of a file number in a name, at least | 6 | @file-numbers
            First file number | 1 | @file-numbers
            Log block size | 32,768 bytes | @log-blocks
            Log record header size | 17 bytes | @log-record-header
            Smallest room in a block for a new record | 18 bytes | @log-blocks
            Largest log record payload | 32,751 bytes | @log-record-header
            Largest block padding | 17 bytes | @log-blocks
            First sequence number assigned | 1 | @log-sequence-numbers
            Sequence numbers are below | 2^56 | @internal-keys
            Batch count field | `uint32`, at least 1 | @log-batch-encoding
            Internal key trailer | 8 bytes: the sequence number shifted left by 8 bits, plus the kind | @internal-keys
            Block trailer, format 4 and later | 5 bytes | @block-trailer
            Block trailer, format 3 | 4 bytes | @format-3
            Data block target size | 16 KiB of entries | @data-blocks
            Restart interval of data and properties blocks | 16 entries | @restart-points
            Restart interval of index blocks | 1 entry | @restart-points
            Snappy kept when it saves at least | One eighth of the block | @compression
            Bloom filter probes | 1 to 30 | @filter-block
            Table footer size | 60 bytes | @sstable-footer
            Table magic | `TIDE-SST`, `0x5453532d45444954` | @sstable-footer
            Manifest edit header size | 12 bytes | @edit-framing
            Tombstone share unit | Parts per 10,000 | @tombstone-share-property
            Time unit of expiry times | Milliseconds since the Unix epoch | @units
        }

        The data block target size is a writer's choice that readers do not depend on; it is listed because the glossary and the architecture both give it as the size of a block.
    }

    # Appendix B: Tag Registry @appendix-tags
    {
        Every enumerated value of the format, with the version that introduced it. A value not listed here is invalid in every version this document covers.

        # Log Record Types @tags-record-types
        {
            {[table]
                Value | Name | Since
                0 | Never a type: padding when fewer than 18 bytes are left in a block |
                1 | `full` | Every version
                2 | `first` | Every version
                3 | `middle` | Every version
                4 | `last` | Every version
                5 to 255 | Reserved |
            }

            Record types are specified in [Write-Ahead Log Format](#write-ahead-log-format).
        }

        # Entry Kinds @tags-entry-kinds
        {
            {[table]
                Value | Name | In tables since | In log batches since
                0 | delete | 3 | Every version
                1 | put | 3 | Every version
                2 | put with expiry | 6 | Releases that write format 6
                3 to 254 | Reserved | |
                255 | Lookup keys only, never stored | |
            }
        }

        # Compression Types @tags-compression
        {
            {[table]
                Value | Name | Since
                0 | none | 4, the only possibility in 3
                1 | Snappy, block format, data blocks only | 4
                2 to 255 | Reserved |
            }
        }

        # Manifest Fields @tags-manifest
        {
            {[table]
                Tag | Field | Value
                1 | `format-version` | Varint, then length-prefixed release
                2 | `log-number` | Varint
                3 | `next-file-number` | Varint
                4 | `last-sequence` | Varint
                5 | `delete-table` | Varint level, varint file number
                6 | `add-table` | Varint level, file number and size, two length-prefixed internal keys, two varint sequence numbers
                7 | `checkpoint` | Varint
                8 | `suspect-table` | Varint file number
            }

            Tag 0 and the tags from 9 up are unassigned. A new tag needs a new format version, since a reader cannot skip a tag it does not know.
        }

        # Table Properties @tags-properties
        {
            {[table]
                Name | Required since | Value
                `tidewater.num-entries` | 3 | Varint count
                `tidewater.num-deletions` | 3 | Varint count
                `tidewater.raw-key-size` | 3 | Varint bytes
                `tidewater.raw-value-size` | 3 | Varint bytes
                `tidewater.smallest-seqno` | 3 | Varint sequence number
                `tidewater.largest-seqno` | 3 | Varint sequence number
                `tidewater.seek-count` | 5 | Varint count
                `tidewater.tombstone-share` | 5 | Varint, parts per 10,000
                `tidewater.num-expiring` | 6 | Varint count
                `tidewater.earliest-expiry` | 6, when any entry has an expiry | Varint milliseconds since the Unix epoch
                `tidewater.bloom-bits-per-key` | Informational | Varint bits
                `tidewater.creation-reason` | Informational | Varint, as in @tags-creation-reasons
                `tidewater.data-size` | Informational | Varint bytes
                `tidewater.num-data-blocks` | Informational | Varint count
            }
        }

        # Creation Reasons @tags-creation-reasons
        {
            {[table]
                Value | Table written by
                1 | A flush
                2 | A compaction, automatic or manual
                3 | `tidewater-admin downgrade`
            }

            Other values are reserved. Since the property is informational, a reader that meets an unknown value reports it as unknown and goes on.
        }

        # Allocating a New Value @tags-allocation
        {
            A new value takes the lowest unassigned number in its registry, never a reserved or retired one, and it is added here and to the section that specifies it in the same change. Values are never reused: a value that falls out of use stays listed, marked retired, so that files written while it was in use stay decodable.
        }
    }

    # Appendix C: Error Codes @appendix-errors
    {
        The codes name the checks of this document. They do not appear in error messages, which name the file, the offset and what was found instead; they are what the format tests and the sections above use to say which check a failure belongs to.

        {[table]
            Code | File | Effect | Wraps `ErrCorrupt` | Section
            `log.short` | Log segment | Replay stops; the segment is good up to the good end | Not returned: logged | @log-validation
            `log.length` | Log segment | As `log.short` | Not returned: logged | @log-validation
            `log.checksum` | Log segment | As `log.short` | Not returned: logged | @log-validation
            `log.fragment` | Log segment | As `log.short` | Not returned: logged | @log-validation
            `log.sequence` | Log segment | As `log.short` | Not returned: logged | @log-validation
            `log.batch` | Log segment | As `log.short` | Not returned: logged | @log-validation
            `log.unreadable` | Log segment | Open fails, nothing truncated | No: the filesystem's error | @log-validation
            `sst.size` | Table | Open fails | Yes | @sstable-validation
            `sst.magic` | Table | Open fails | Yes | @sstable-validation
            `sst.version` | Table | Open fails | No | @refusing-to-open
            `sst.handle` | Table | Open or read fails | Yes | @sstable-validation
            `sst.checksum` | Table | The read fails; the table is marked suspect | Yes | @block-trailer
            `sst.compression` | Table | The read fails | Yes | @compression
            `sst.snappy` | Table | The read fails | Yes | @compression
            `sst.block` | Table | The read fails | Yes | @data-blocks
            `sst.order` | Table | The read fails | Yes | @data-blocks
            `sst.key` | Table | The read fails | Yes | @internal-keys
            `sst.filter` | Table | Open fails | Yes | @filter-block
            `sst.property` | Table | Open fails | Yes | @properties-block
            `current.missing` | `CURRENT` | Open fails | Yes | @current-file
            `current.syntax` | `CURRENT` | Open fails | Yes | @current-file
            `manifest.missing` | Manifest | Open fails | Yes | @current-file
            `manifest.checksum` | Manifest | Open fails | Yes | @manifest-torn-tail
            `manifest.tag` | Manifest | Open fails | Yes | @manifest-fields
            `manifest.field` | Manifest | Open fails | Yes | @manifest-fields
            `manifest.state` | Manifest | Open fails | Yes | @manifest-replay-rules
            `manifest.version` | Manifest | Open fails | No | @refusing-to-open
            `manifest.table-missing` | Manifest and table | Open fails | Yes | @manifest-replay-rules
            `format.downgrade` | Any | `tidewater-admin downgrade` refuses | Not returned by the API | @downgrade
        }

        The log codes are never returned by `Open`: a bad record is the end of the log, not an error, and the database opens with every batch before it. They appear in the log line of @log-torn-tail and in the output of `tidewater-admin wal verify`.

        A table error at open makes `Open` fail; the same error on a later read fails only that read. Errors that wrap `ErrCorrupt` are matched with `errors.Is(err, tidewater.ErrCorrupt)`. The version refusals wrap no sentinel of the package: they are not damage, and no retry or repair helps, only another release.
    }

    # Appendix D: Test Vectors @appendix-test-vectors
    {
        Every vector below was produced by the encoder this document specifies and checked against the engine's decoders. Hex strings are the exact bytes, in file order. A reader that decodes them as given, and a writer that reproduces them byte for byte from the inputs, agree with the format.

        # Checksums @vectors-checksums
        {
            {[table]
                Function | Input | Output
                CRC32C | `123456789` | `0xe3069283`
                CRC32C | 32 bytes of `00` | `0x8a9136aa`
                CRC32C | 32 bytes of `ff` | `0x62a8ab43`
                CRC32C | The bytes `00` to `1f` | `0x46dd794e`
                xxh3-64 | No bytes | `0x2d06800538d394c2`
                xxh3-64 | `123456789` | `0x72dcb18b67a17dff`
                xxh3-64 | `tidewater` | `0x42618d12d77a2315`
                xxh3-64 | `user:1005` | `0xfadb677fa84a8351`
            }
        }

        # Varints @vectors-varints
        {
            {[table]
                Value | Encoding
                0 | `00`
                127 | `7f`
                128 | `80 01`
                951 | `b7 07`
                40,000 | `c0 b8 02`
                1,284,705 | `e1 b4 4e`
                1,900,000,000,000 | `80 f0 cc 86 a6 37`
                2^64 − 1 | `ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff 01`
            }
        }

        # Log Records @vectors-log
        {
            A full record, sequence number 1, a put of `key1` to `value1`, from @log-example-full:

            ```
            fa f0 16 68 11 00 00 00 01 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 04 6b 65 79 31 06 76 61 6c 75 65 31
            ```

            A full record, sequence number 2, a put of `key2` to `value2`:

            ```
            c0 d8 5f d2 11 00 00 00 01 02 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 04 6b 65 79 32 06 76 61 6c 75 65 32
            ```

            A full record, sequence number 1,000, three entries, from @log-example-batch:

            ```
            40 48 ff 3c 32 00 00 00 01 e8 03 00 00 00 00 00 00 03 00 00 00 01 07 75 73 65 72 3a 34 32
            05 61 6c 69 63 65 00 06 75 73 65 72 3a 37 02 09 73 65 73 73 69 6f 6e 3a 39 00 38 d3 60 ba
            01 00 00 03 74 6f 6b
            ```

            The three headers of the fragmented batch of @log-example-fragmented, whose payload is `01 00 00 00 01 03 62 69 67 c0 b8 02` followed by 40,000 bytes of `78`:

            {[table]
                Offset | Header
                32,700 | `96 be 46 be 33 00 00 00 02 88 13 00 00 00 00 00 00`
                32,768 | `4d 7c d7 7b ef 7f 00 00 03 88 13 00 00 00 00 00 00`
                65,536 | `f3 a0 26 e3 2a 1c 00 00 04 88 13 00 00 00 00 00 00`
            }
        }

        # Internal Keys @vectors-internal-keys
        {
            {[table]
                User key | Sequence number | Kind | Internal key
                `user:1005` | 1,284,705 | 1 | `75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 35 01 61 9a 13 00 00 00 00`
                `user:1006` | 1,284,706 | 0 | `75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 36 00 62 9a 13 00 00 00 00`
                `user:1017` | 1,284,717 | 2 | `75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 31 37 02 6d 9a 13 00 00 00 00`
                `user:1100` | 1,284,733 | 1 | `75 73 65 72 3a 31 31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00`
                `user:1017`, lookup at 1,284,720 | 1,284,720 | 255 | `75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 31 37 ff 70 9a 13 00 00 00 00`
            }
        }

        # Filter Probes @vectors-filter
        {
            For a filter of 48 bits with 8 probes, as in @filter-example:

            {[table]
                User key | Probe positions
                `user:1005` | 1, 16, 31, 46, 13, 28, 43, 10
                `user:1006` | 32, 7, 30, 5, 28, 3, 26, 1
                `user:1017` | 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19
                `user:1100` | 40, 35, 30, 25, 20, 15, 10, 5
                `user:1003` | 39, 45, 3, 9, 15, 21, 27, 33
                `user:1002` | 43, eight times
            }

            The resulting filter block, before its trailer, is `aa ae 1b d6 09 49 08`.
        }

        # Snappy @vectors-snappy
        {
            {[table]
                Uncompressed | Compressed
                `abababababababab`, 16 bytes | `10 04 61 62 36 02 00`
            }
        }

        # A Whole Table @vectors-table
        {
            The 456-byte table of @sstable-example, from the entries of @data-block-example and a filter built with 12 bits per key. Its blocks, with their trailers:

            {[table]
                Block | Handle | Trailer
                Data | Offset 0, 88 bytes | `00 4f d0 c5 8f`
                Filter | Offset 93, 7 bytes | `00 0e 29 f4 a8`
                Properties | Offset 105, 251 bytes | `00 4a fd 37 68`
                Index | Offset 361, 30 bytes | `00 05 a2 5c 7b`
            }

            The CRC32C of the whole file is `0xed93652e`. An encoder that produces a file with another checksum from the same entries differs from this document somewhere, and the trailers above say in which block.
        }

        # Manifest Edits @vectors-manifest
        {
            The first edit of a new database:

            ```
            0c 00 00 00 97 30 eb 53 96 8d f3 ae 01 06 03 32 2e 33 02 01 03 03 04 00
            ```

            The flush edit of `sst/000951.sst`, from @manifest-example:

            ```
            3a 00 00 00 a2 6b aa 99 3f 6b e1 21 06 00 b7 07 c8 03 11 75 73 65 72 3a 31 30 30 35 01 61
            9a 13 00 00 00 00 11 75 73 65 72 3a 31 31 30 30 01 7d 9a 13 00 00 00 00 e1 b4 4e fd b4 4e
            02 b6 07 03 b8 07 04 fd b4 4e
            ```

            A checkpoint edit at 1,284,733, a `suspect-table` edit for file 951, and the recovery checkpoint of @recovery-checkpoint:

            ```
            04 00 00 00 35 49 4f 28 af 98 1d 58 07 fd b4 4e
            03 00 00 00 6e 8e 30 dd a0 f9 dc 33 08 b7 07
            07 00 00 00 94 9a 1b 6b 01 0b d3 e8 07 89 bf 4f 02 bd 07
            ```

            The `CURRENT` file naming `MANIFEST-000042`:

            ```
            4d 41 4e 49 46 45 53 54 2d 30 30 30 30 34 32 0a
            ```
        }

        # Negative Vectors @vectors-negative
        {
            Each input below is a valid vector of this appendix with one change. A compatible reader reports the code given, and nothing else.

            {[table]
                Input | Change | Expected
                The segment of @log-example-torn | None: it is already cut 20 bytes into its second record | `log.short` at offset 34, good end 34
                The two log records of sequence numbers 1 and 2, back to back | Byte 30 of the second record, in its value, has its low bit flipped | `log.checksum` at offset 34, stored `0xd25fd8c0`, computed `0x0f1a7278`, good end 34
                The same two records | The second record's length set to `00 80 00 00` | `log.length` at offset 34, good end 34
                The same two records | The second record's sequence number set to 3, and its checksum recomputed | `log.sequence` at offset 34, good end 34
                The fragmented batch | The segment ends at offset 65,536, after the `middle` | `log.short` at offset 32,700, the `first` fragment, good end 32,700
                The table of @vectors-table | The last 8 bytes set to zero | `sst.magic`
                The same table | The footer's version set to 7 | `sst.version`, not wrapping `ErrCorrupt`
                The same table | Byte 0 of the data block changed | `sst.checksum` on the first read of the block; open succeeds
                The same table | The filter block's probe count set to 0, and its trailer recomputed | `sst.filter`
                The same table | The compression byte of the filter block's trailer set to 1, and the trailer's checksum recomputed | `sst.compression`
                The first edit of a new database, followed by the flush edit | The flush edit cut after its first 30 bytes | No error: the flush edit is ignored as a torn tail
                The same two edits | A byte of the first edit's body changed | `manifest.checksum` at offset 0
                The checkpoint edit appended after a first edit | Its body changed to `17 fd b4 4e`, and its checksum recomputed | `manifest.tag`
                The `CURRENT` file | Its final newline removed | `current.syntax`
            }
        }
    }

    # Appendix E: Field Index @appendix-field-index
    {
        Every named field of every structure, in alphabetical order, with the structure that holds it and the section that specifies it.

        {[table]
            Field | Structure | Section
            `add-table` | Manifest edit, tag 6 | @add-table-field
            bits | Filter block | @filter-block
            `checkpoint` | Manifest edit, tag 7 | @checkpoint-encoding
            checksum | Block trailer | @block-trailer
            checksum | Manifest edit header | @edit-framing
            compression | Block trailer | @block-trailer
            count | Log batch | @log-batch-encoding
            crc32c | Log record header | @log-record-header
            `delete-table` | Manifest edit, tag 5 | @manifest-fields
            expiry | Log batch entry, kind 2 | @log-batch-encoding
            expiry | Data block value, kind 2 | @expiring-values
            filter offset, filter size | Table footer | @sstable-footer
            format version | Table footer | @sstable-footer
            `format-version` | Manifest edit, tag 1 | @manifest-fields
            index offset, index size | Table footer | @sstable-footer
            key | Log batch entry | @log-batch-encoding
            key suffix | Block entry | @data-block-entries
            kind | Log batch entry | @log-batch-encoding
            kind | Internal key trailer | @internal-keys
            `last-sequence` | Manifest edit, tag 4 | @manifest-fields
            length | Log record header | @log-record-header
            length | Manifest edit header | @edit-framing
            `log-number` | Manifest edit, tag 2 | @manifest-fields
            magic | Table footer | @sstable-footer
            `next-file-number` | Manifest edit, tag 3 | @manifest-fields
            payload | Log record | @log-record-header
            probes | Filter block | @filter-block
            properties offset, properties size | Table footer | @sstable-footer
            restart count | Block | @restart-points
            restart offsets | Block | @restart-points
            seqno | Log record header | @log-sequence-numbers
            seqno | Internal key trailer | @internal-keys
            shared | Block entry | @data-block-entries
            `suspect-table` | Manifest edit, tag 8 | @manifest-fields
            type | Log record header | @log-record-types
            unshared | Block entry | @data-block-entries
            value | Log batch entry | @log-batch-encoding
            value | Block entry | @data-block-entries
            value length | Block entry | @data-block-entries
        }
    }

    # Appendix F: Common Mistakes @appendix-mistakes
    {
        Mistakes that implementations of this format have made, each with the check of this document that catches it, so that a reader that fails a vector can find the cause quickly.

        {[table]
            Mistake | What it breaks | Caught by
            CRC-32 with the IEEE polynomial instead of CRC32C | Every log record and every block | The check values of @crc32c
            Masking or rotating the CRC32C before storing it, as some other storage engines do | Every log record and every block | The vectors of @vectors-log
            Computing a log record's CRC32C over the length field too | Every log record | @log-example-full
            Leaving the compression byte out of a block's CRC32C | Every block of format 4 and later | @vectors-table
            Taking every trailer as 5 bytes, or every trailer as 4 | Every block of the other format | @trailer-versions-example
            Big-endian integers | Everything | @byte-order
            Treating a log record's length as including its header | Every log record | @log-record-header
            Moving a batch to the next block instead of fragmenting it | Byte-for-byte reproduction of logs | @log-example-fragmented
            Resynchronising after a bad log record and replaying what follows | The order of visibility | @log-torn-tail
            Replaying segments sorted as strings | Logs past file number 999,999 | @file-numbers
            Applying a manifest edit before verifying its checksum | A partly applied edit | @edit-framing
            Truncating a manifest at a bad edit that is not at its tail | A version that never existed | @manifest-torn-tail
            Skipping an unknown manifest tag | Everything after it in the edit | @manifest-fields
            Comparing internal keys bytewise, trailer included | The order of versions of a key | @internal-keys
            Looking up a key with kind 0 in the lookup key | The newest version at the read's sequence number | @block-search
            Hashing the internal key into the filter instead of the user key | Every filter query | @filter-building
            Computing the probe count from the bits per key instead of reading it | Filters built under another setting | @filter-block
            Requiring the properties of format 5 in a format 4 table | Every table written before 2.1 | @required-properties
            Rejecting unknown property names | Tables written by a later release | @informational-properties
            Reading the expiry after the value instead of before it | Every entry of kind 2 | @expiring-values
            Taking an expired put for a missing key and looking further down | Keys whose older versions are still in deeper levels | @expiring-values
            Opening `sst/NNNNNN.sst.tmp` files, or tables no edit names | The set of live tables | @orphans
        }
    }

    # Appendix G: Implementation Notes in Go @appendix-go
    {
        Sketches of the checks this document asks for, in the style of the engine's own code. They leave out error wrapping and reporting, and are no substitute for the sections they come from.

        # Verifying a Block @go-block
        {
            ```go
            // readBlock returns the decoded contents of the block at h,
            // for a table of format version v.
            func readBlock(f io.ReaderAt, h handle, v uint32, data bool) ([]byte, error) {
                trailer := 5
                if v == 3 {
                    trailer = 4
                }
                buf := make([]byte, h.size+uint64(trailer))
                if _, err := f.ReadAt(buf, int64(h.offset)); err != nil {
                    return nil, err
                }
                stored := binary.LittleEndian.Uint32(buf[len(buf)-4:])
                if crc32.Checksum(buf[:len(buf)-4], castagnoli) != stored {
                    return nil, corrupt("sst.checksum")
                }
                if v == 3 {
                    return buf[:h.size], nil
                }
                switch buf[h.size] {
                case 0:
                    return buf[:h.size], nil
                case 1:
                    if !data {
                        return nil, corrupt("sst.compression")
                    }
                    return decodeSnappy(buf[:h.size]) // sst.snappy on failure
                default:
                    return nil, corrupt("sst.compression")
                }
            }
            ```

            The checksum runs over the block and, from format 4 on, its compression byte, which sit side by side in `buf`: one call covers both.
        }

        # Probing a Filter @go-filter
        {
            ```go
            // mayContain reports whether the filter block f, trailer removed,
            // may hold userKey.
            func mayContain(f []byte, userKey []byte) bool {
                bits, k := f[:len(f)-1], uint64(f[len(f)-1])
                m := uint64(len(bits)) * 8
                h := xxh3.Hash(userKey)
                lo, hi := h&0xffffffff, h>>32
                for i := uint64(0); i < k; i++ {
                    b := (lo + i*hi) % m
                    if bits[b/8]&(1<<(b%8)) == 0 {
                        return false
                    }
                }
                return true
            }
            ```
        }

        # Reading Manifest Edits @go-manifest
        {
            ```go
            // nextEdit returns the body of the edit at off and the offset after it.
            // torn is true when the edit is bad and nothing follows it.
            func nextEdit(m []byte, off int) (body []byte, next int, torn bool, err error) {
                if len(m)-off < 12 {
                    return nil, 0, true, nil // a header cut short is always at the tail
                }
                n := int(binary.LittleEndian.Uint32(m[off:]))
                end := off + 12 + n
                if n == 0 || end > len(m) {
                    if n != 0 {
                        return nil, 0, true, nil // a body cut short is always at the tail
                    }
                    return nil, 0, false, corrupt("manifest.field")
                }
                body = m[off+12 : end]
                if xxh3.Hash(body) != binary.LittleEndian.Uint64(m[off+4:]) {
                    if end == len(m) {
                        return nil, 0, true, nil // bad, with nothing after it: torn
                    }
                    return nil, 0, false, corrupt("manifest.checksum")
                }
                return body, end, false, nil
            }
            ```

            A torn result ends the replay with the version as it stands. The engine then truncates the file at `off` before its next append; a tool leaves it alone.
        }
    }
}
